window.BAHR_DATA=window.BAHR_DATA||{};window.BAHR_DATA["passages-12"]=[{"id":"q2-204-207","q":"2:204–207","s":2,"surah":"Al-Baqara","title":"2:204–207 · Pleasing speech, destructive conduct and the self sold to God","source":"Cairo print · Volume I, pp. 233–235","cairo":"I:233–235","source_page_first":233,"source_page_last":235,"ar":"وَمِنَ النَّاسِ مَنْ يُعْجِبُكَ قَوْلُهُ فِي الْحَياةِ الدُّنْيا وَيُشْهِدُ اللَّهَ عَلى ما فِي قَلْبِهِ وَهُوَ أَلَدُّ الْخِصامِ (204) وَإِذا تَوَلَّى سَعى فِي الْأَرْضِ لِيُفْسِدَ فِيها وَيُهْلِكَ الْحَرْثَ وَالنَّسْلَ وَاللَّهُ لا يُحِبُّ الْفَسادَ (205) وَإِذا قِيلَ لَهُ اتَّقِ اللَّهَ أَخَذَتْهُ الْعِزَّةُ بِالْإِثْمِ فَحَسْبُهُ جَهَنَّمُ وَلَبِئْسَ الْمِهادُ (206) وَمِنَ النَّاسِ مَنْ يَشْرِي نَفْسَهُ ابْتِغاءَ مَرْضاتِ اللَّهِ وَاللَّهُ رَؤُفٌ بِالْعِبادِ (207)\nقلت: نزلت الآية في الأخنَسْ بن شريق الثقفي وصُهيب بن سنان الرومي، أما الأخنس فكان رجلاً حسن المنظر، حُلُو المنطق، كان يوالي رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم ويدَّعي الإسلام، ثم ارتد، ومرَّ على زرع وحُمر للمسلمين فقتلها وأفسد الزرع، قال ابن عطية: ولم يثبت أنه أسلم. قلت: بل ذكره في القاموس من الصحابة، فانظره، ولعله تاب بعد نزول الآية. وأما صهيب الرومي فأخذه المشركون وعذبوه ليرتد، فقال لهم: إني شيخ كبير لا أنفعكم إن كنت معكم، ولا أضركم إن كنت عليكم، فخلّوني وما أنا عليه، وخذوا مالي، فقبلوه منه، وأتى المدينة فلما رآه صلّى الله عليه وسلم قال له: «رَبِحْتَ يا أَبَا يَحْيى» .\nوقيل: نزلت في المنافقين ومَنْ نحَا نحوهم، وفيمن باع نفسه لله في الجهاد وتغيير المنكر من المسلمين. و (في الحياة الدنيا) يتعلق بالقول، و (ألد الخصام) شديده، وفي الحديث: «أبْغَضُ الرِّجالِ إلى اللهِ الألدُّ الخَصِم» .\nوالخِصام: مصدر، أو جمع خصيم.\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: وَمِنَ النَّاسِ قوم حلو اللسان خرَاب الجَنَان، إذا تكلم في شأن الدنيا يُعْجِبُكَ قَوْلُهُ فيها لرونقه وفصاحته، وَيُشْهِدُ اللَّهَ أي: يحلف على أنه موافق لقلبه، وأن ظاهره موافق لباطنه، وهو شديد الخصومة والعداوة للمسلمين، أو أشد الخصوم، وَإِذا تَوَلَّى أي: أدبر وانصرف عنك، سَعى فِي الْأَرْضِ أي: مشى فيها بنية الإفساد لِيُفْسِدَ فِيها وَيُهْلِكَ الْحَرْثَ وَالنَّسْلَ كما فعل الأخنَسْ، أو كما فعله أهل الظلم، فيَحْبِسُ الله القَطْر، فَيَهْلِكَ الحرثُ والنسل بشؤم معاصيهم، وَاللَّهُ لا يُحِبُّ الْفَسادَ أي: لا يرتضيه، فاحذروا غضبه. وَإِذا قِيلَ لَهُ اتَّقِ اللَّهَ وارجع عما أنت عليه من الفساد أَخَذَتْهُ الْعِزَّةُ أي: حملته الحمية والأنفة بسبب الإثم الذي ارتكبه، فلا ينزجر عن غَيِّه. أو حملَتْه الحمية على الإثم الذي يُؤمَر باتقائه. فَحَسْبُهُ جَهَنَّمُ أي: كَفَته عذاباً وعقاباً، وهي عَلَمٌ لدار العقاب، كالنار، وَلَبِئْسَ الْمِهادُ هي، أي: بئس الفراش الذي مهَّده لنفسه.\nونزل في مقابله، وهو صهيب، أو كل من بذل نفسه لله: وَمِنَ النَّاسِ مَنْ يَشْرِي نَفْسَهُ أي: يبيعُها ويبذلها لله في الجهاد وغيره، ابْتِغاءَ مَرْضاتِ اللَّهِ والوصول إلى حضرته وَاللَّهُ رَؤُفٌ بِالْعِبادِ الذين يفعلون مثل هذا، فيدرأ عنهم المضَارّ، ويجلبُ لهم المَسَار أينما حَلُّوا من الدارين.\nالإشارة: الناس على قسمين: قسم زَيّنُوا ظواهرهم وخرَّبوا بواطنهم، ظاهرهم جميل وباطنُهم قبيح، إذا تكلموا في الدنيا أو في الحس، أعجبَك قولهم، وراقك منظرُهم، وإذا تكلموا في الآخرة، أو في المعنى، أخذتهم الحبْسةُ والدهشة. وفي بعض الكتب المنزلة: «إنَّ مِنْ عِبَاد الله قوماً ألْسنَتُهُم أحْلَى منَ العَسَلِ، وَقُلُوبُهُمْ أمرّ مِنْ الصَّبْرِ، يَلْبَسُونَ للنَّاس جُلُودَ الضَّأنِ مِن اللِّينِ، يَجْتَرُّون الدُّنْيَا بالدين، يَقُولُ الله تعالى: أبِي يَغْتَرُّونَ، وعليَّ يَجْترِئُون؟ حَلَفتْ لأُسلطنَّ عليهمْ فتْنةٌ تَدَعُ الْحَليم مِنْهُمْ حَيْرَانَ» .\nوقوله (يلبسون ... ) الخ. كناية عن إظهار اللين والسهول ليخدع ويغر الناس ليتوصل إلى حظ نفسه من الدنيا، ومع ذلك يدعي موافقة ظاهره لباطنه، وهو شديد الخصومة لأهل الله، وإذا تولى عنك اشتغل بالمعاصي والذنوب، ليُفسد في الأرض، ويهلك الحرث والنسل بشؤم معاصيه، وإذا ذُكِّر: أنِفَ واستكبر، وأخذته حمية الجاهلية، فحسبُه البُعد في نار القطيعة.\nوالقسم الثاني: قوم زَيَّنوا بواطنهم وخربوا ظواهرهم، عمّروا قلوبهم بمحبة الله، وبذلوا أنفسهم في مرضات الله، قلوبهم في أعلى عليين، وأشباحهم في أسفل سافلين، فأولئك المقربون مع النبيين والمرسلين. قال بعض العارفين:\nكلما وضعت نفسك أرضاً أرضاً، سما قلبك سماء سماء، وكل ما نقص من حسك زاد في معناك. وفي الحديث:\n«مَن تواضعَ دُون قَدْره رَفَعهُ الله فوقَ قَدْره» . وبالله التوفيق.\nثم دعا الحق، تعالى عباده، إلى التوغل فى الإسلام، فقال:","quran_ar":"وَمِنَ النَّاسِ مَنْ يُعْجِبُكَ قَوْلُهُ فِي الْحَياةِ الدُّنْيا وَيُشْهِدُ اللَّهَ عَلى ما فِي قَلْبِهِ وَهُوَ أَلَدُّ الْخِصامِ (204) وَإِذا تَوَلَّى سَعى فِي الْأَرْضِ لِيُفْسِدَ فِيها وَيُهْلِكَ الْحَرْثَ وَالنَّسْلَ وَاللَّهُ لا يُحِبُّ الْفَسادَ (205) وَإِذا قِيلَ لَهُ اتَّقِ اللَّهَ أَخَذَتْهُ الْعِزَّةُ بِالْإِثْمِ فَحَسْبُهُ جَهَنَّمُ وَلَبِئْسَ الْمِهادُ (206) وَمِنَ النَّاسِ مَنْ يَشْرِي نَفْسَهُ ابْتِغاءَ مَرْضاتِ اللَّهِ وَاللَّهُ رَؤُفٌ بِالْعِبادِ (207)","quran_en":"204. Among people is one whose speech about worldly life pleases you, and who calls God to witness what is in his heart, although he is the fiercest of adversaries. 205. When he turns away, he strives through the land to spread corruption in it and destroy crops and offspring. God does not love corruption. 206. When he is told, “Fear God,” pride carries him into sin. Hell is enough for him—an evil resting place! 207. Among people is one who sells himself in pursuit of God’s good pleasure. God is tender towards His servants.","segments":[{"type":"language","title":"Al-Akhnas and Ṣuhayb","html":"<p>I say: the verses were revealed concerning al-Akhnas ibn Sharīq al-Thaqafī and Ṣuhayb ibn Sinān al-Rūmī. Al-Akhnas was a man of handsome appearance and sweet speech. He professed friendship towards God’s Messenger, God bless him and grant him peace, and claimed Islam. He then apostatized and, passing Muslim crops and donkeys, killed the animals and ruined the crops. Ibn ʿAṭiyya says: “It has not been established that he embraced Islam.” I say: rather, the <i>Qāmūs<\/i> lists him among the Companions; consult it. Perhaps he repented after the verse’s revelation.<\/p><p>As for Ṣuhayb al-Rūmī, the associators seized and tortured him to make him turn back. He told them: “I am an old man: I will not benefit you if I am with you or harm you if I am against you. Leave me and the faith I follow, and take my wealth.” They accepted. He came to Medina, and when the Prophet, blessings and peace be upon him, saw him, he said: “Your bargain has prospered, Abū Yaḥyā!”<a class=\"source-marker\" href=\"Source_Reference_Ledger_XII.html#LEGACY-144\" title=\"Inherited reference occurrence; see current source status\">[B139]<\/a><\/p><p>It is also said that the verses concern the hypocrites and those who follow their way, and Muslims who sell themselves to God in fighting in His path and changing what is wrong. “In worldly life” is attached to “speech.” “Fiercest in disputation” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">ألد الخصام<\/bdi>) means intensely contentious. The Tradition says: “The man most hated by God is the stubborn, contentious adversary.” <i>Khiṣām<\/i> is either a verbal noun (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">مصدر<\/bdi>) or the plural of <i>khaṣīm<\/i>, an adversary.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"I say: the verses were revealed concerning al-Akhnas ibn Sharīq al-Thaqafī and Ṣuhayb ibn Sinān al-Rūmī. Al-Akhnas was a man of handsome appearance and sweet speech. He professed friendship towards God’s Messenger, God bless him and grant him peace, and claimed Islam. He then apostatized and, passing Muslim crops and donkeys, killed the animals and ruined the crops. Ibn ʿAṭiyya says: “It has not been established that he embraced Islam.” I say: rather, the Qāmūs lists him among the Companions; consult it. Perhaps he repented after the verse’s revelation. As for Ṣuhayb al-Rūmī, the associators seized and tortured him to make him turn back. He told them: “I am an old man: I will not benefit you if I am with you or harm you if I am against you. Leave me and the faith I follow, and take my wealth.” They accepted. He came to Medina, and when the Prophet, blessings and peace be upon him, saw him, he said: “Your bargain has prospered, Abū Yaḥyā!” [B139] It is also said that the verses concern the hypocrites and those who follow their way, and Muslims who sell themselves to God in fighting in His path and changing what is wrong. “In worldly life” is attached to “speech.” “Fiercest in disputation” ( ألد الخصام ) means intensely contentious. The Tradition says: “The man most hated by God is the stubborn, contentious adversary.” Khiṣām is either a verbal noun ( مصدر ) or the plural of khaṣīm , an adversary."},{"type":"exposition","title":"Eloquence without inward soundness","html":"<p>The Real, glorious is His majesty, says: among people are those whose tongues are sweet and whose hearts are ruined. When one speaks of worldly affairs, his speech pleases you through its polish and eloquence. He calls God to witness: he swears that it agrees with his heart and that his exterior agrees with his interior, although he is fiercely contentious and hostile towards Muslims—or the fiercest of adversaries.<\/p><p>When he turns away, departing from you, he goes through the land intending corruption, to spread corruption in it and destroy crops and offspring, as al-Akhnas did; or as the unjust do, whereupon God withholds rain and crops and offspring perish through the ill consequence of their sins. God does not love corruption: He does not approve it. Beware of His wrath.<\/p><p>When he is told, “Fear God and turn back from your corruption,” pride seizes him: indignation and disdain, because of the sin he has committed, prevent him from leaving his error. Or indignation drives him into the sin he is commanded to avoid. Hell suffices him as punishment and torment. <i>Jahannam<\/i> is a proper name (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">عَلَم<\/bdi>) for the abode of punishment, like “the Fire.” What an evil resting place it is: an evil bed he has prepared for himself.<\/p><p>Concerning his opposite—Ṣuhayb, or everyone who gives himself to God—it was revealed: among people is one who sells himself, offering himself to God in fighting in His path and otherwise, seeking God’s good pleasure and arrival in His presence. God is tender towards servants who act thus: He averts harms from them and brings them joys wherever they dwell in either abode.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real, glorious is His majesty, says: among people are those whose tongues are sweet and whose hearts are ruined. When one speaks of worldly affairs, his speech pleases you through its polish and eloquence. He calls God to witness: he swears that it agrees with his heart and that his exterior agrees with his interior, although he is fiercely contentious and hostile towards Muslims—or the fiercest of adversaries. When he turns away, departing from you, he goes through the land intending corruption, to spread corruption in it and destroy crops and offspring, as al-Akhnas did; or as the unjust do, whereupon God withholds rain and crops and offspring perish through the ill consequence of their sins. God does not love corruption: He does not approve it. Beware of His wrath. When he is told, “Fear God and turn back from your corruption,” pride seizes him: indignation and disdain, because of the sin he has committed, prevent him from leaving his error. Or indignation drives him into the sin he is commanded to avoid. Hell suffices him as punishment and torment. Jahannam is a proper name ( عَلَم ) for the abode of punishment, like “the Fire.” What an evil resting place it is: an evil bed he has prepared for himself. Concerning his opposite—Ṣuhayb, or everyone who gives himself to God—it was revealed: among people is one who sells himself, offering himself to God in fighting in His path and otherwise, seeking God’s good pleasure and arrival in His presence. God is tender towards servants who act thus: He averts harms from them and brings them joys wherever they dwell in either abode."},{"type":"spiritual","title":"Adorning the exterior or cultivating the heart","html":"<p>People are of two kinds. One kind adorn their exteriors and ruin their interiors: outwardly beautiful, inwardly ugly. If they speak about the world or sensory appearances, their speech pleases you and their appearance delights you. If they speak of the Hereafter or inward meaning, they are seized by speechlessness and bewilderment. One of the revealed books says:<\/p><p>“Among God’s servants are people whose tongues are sweeter than honey and whose hearts are more bitter than aloes. They dress before people in the softness of sheepskins and draw the world to themselves through religion. God Most High says: ‘Are they deceived about Me, and do they dare defy Me? I have sworn to set over them a trial that will leave even their forbearing ones bewildered.’”<a class=\"source-marker\" href=\"Source_Reference_Ledger_XII.html#LEGACY-145\" title=\"Inherited reference occurrence; see current source status\">[B140]<\/a><\/p><p>“Dress before people,” and the remainder, is an allusion (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">كناية<\/bdi>) to displaying gentleness and easy manners in order to deceive and delude people and reach one’s own worldly interest. At the same time, such a person claims that his exterior agrees with his interior, although he is fiercely contentious towards God’s folk. When he turns away from you, he occupies himself with sins and disobedience, spreading corruption in the earth and destroying crops and offspring through his sins’ ill consequence. When reminded, he disdains and grows proud, seized by the indignation of ignorance. Distance in the fire of separation suffices him.<\/p><p>The second kind adorn their interiors and lay their exteriors waste. They cultivate their hearts with God’s love and give themselves for His good pleasure. Their hearts are in the highest heights, their bodily forms in the lowest depths. They are the ones brought near, with the prophets and messengers. A gnostic said: “The more you lower yourself, earth upon earth, the higher your heart rises, heaven upon heaven. Whatever diminishes from your sensory appearance increases in your inward meaning.” The Tradition says: “Whoever humbles himself below his rank, God raises above his rank.” And success is through God.<a class=\"source-marker\" href=\"Source_Reference_Ledger_XII.html#LEGACY-146\" title=\"Inherited reference occurrence; see current source status\">[B141]<\/a><\/p>","authorial":true,"text":"People are of two kinds. One kind adorn their exteriors and ruin their interiors: outwardly beautiful, inwardly ugly. If they speak about the world or sensory appearances, their speech pleases you and their appearance delights you. If they speak of the Hereafter or inward meaning, they are seized by speechlessness and bewilderment. One of the revealed books says: “Among God’s servants are people whose tongues are sweeter than honey and whose hearts are more bitter than aloes. They dress before people in the softness of sheepskins and draw the world to themselves through religion. God Most High says: ‘Are they deceived about Me, and do they dare defy Me? I have sworn to set over them a trial that will leave even their forbearing ones bewildered.’” [B140] “Dress before people,” and the remainder, is an allusion ( كناية ) to displaying gentleness and easy manners in order to deceive and delude people and reach one’s own worldly interest. At the same time, such a person claims that his exterior agrees with his interior, although he is fiercely contentious towards God’s folk. When he turns away from you, he occupies himself with sins and disobedience, spreading corruption in the earth and destroying crops and offspring through his sins’ ill consequence. When reminded, he disdains and grows proud, seized by the indignation of ignorance. Distance in the fire of separation suffices him. The second kind adorn their interiors and lay their exteriors waste. They cultivate their hearts with God’s love and give themselves for His good pleasure. Their hearts are in the highest heights, their bodily forms in the lowest depths. They are the ones brought near, with the prophets and messengers. A gnostic said: “The more you lower yourself, earth upon earth, the higher your heart rises, heaven upon heaven. Whatever diminishes from your sensory appearance increases in your inward meaning.” The Tradition says: “Whoever humbles himself below his rank, God raises above his rank.” And success is through God. [B141]"},{"type":"transition","title":"","html":"<p>The Real then calls His servants to enter deeply into Islam.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real then calls His servants to enter deeply into Islam."}],"cairo_notes":[],"english_characters":5107,"arabic_characters":3775},{"id":"q2-208-209","q":"2:208–209","s":2,"surah":"Al-Baqara","title":"2:208–209 · Entering wholly into submission","source":"Cairo print · Volume I, pp. 235–236","cairo":"I:235–236","source_page_first":235,"source_page_last":236,"ar":"يا أَيُّهَا الَّذِينَ آمَنُوا ادْخُلُوا فِي السِّلْمِ كَافَّةً وَلا تَتَّبِعُوا خُطُواتِ الشَّيْطانِ إِنَّهُ لَكُمْ عَدُوٌّ مُبِينٌ (208) فَإِنْ زَلَلْتُمْ مِنْ بَعْدِ ما جاءَتْكُمُ الْبَيِّناتُ فَاعْلَمُوا أَنَّ اللَّهَ عَزِيزٌ حَكِيمٌ (209)\nقلت: (السلم) ، بالفتح والكسر: هو الاستسلام والانقياد، ويبعد هنا تفسيره بالصُّلْح. و (كافة) : حال من الواو والسلم معاً، كقوله تعالى: فَأَتَتْ بِهِ قَوْمَها تَحْمِلُهُ.\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: يا أَيُّهَا الَّذِينَ آمَنُوا بمحمد صلى الله عليه وسلم من أهل الكتاب ادْخُلُوا فِي شرائع الإسلام كَافَّةً بحيث لا تهملوا شيئاً منها، ولا تلتفتوا إلى غيرها. نزلت في عبد الله بن سلام وأصحابه، حيث دخلوا فى الإسلام، وأرادوا أن يُعظّموا السبت، وتحرجوا من لحوم الإبل. أو في المنافقين حيث أسلموا في الظاهر، ونافقوا في الباطن، فقال لهم الحق جلّ جلاله: يا أَيُّهَا الَّذِينَ آمَنُوا في الظاهر، ادخلوا في الإسلام كَافَّةً ظاهراً وباطناً. أو في المسلمين يأمرهم بالتمسك بشرائع الإسلام كلها، والبحث عن أحكامها وأسرارها، وَلا تَتَّبِعُوا خُطُواتِ الشَّيْطانِ أي: طُرُقَه الدالة على التفريق والتفرق إِنَّهُ لَكُمْ عَدُوٌّ مُبِينٌ أي: بيّن العداوة.\nفَإِنْ زَلَلْتُمْ عن طريق الجادَّة ففرقتم بين أجزاء الشريعة، أو التفتُّم إلى غير شريعتكم، مِنْ بَعْدِ ما جاءَتْكُمُ الآيات الْبَيِّناتُ الدالة على صحة الدين ونبوة محمد صلّى الله عليه وسلم، فَاعْلَمُوا أَنَّ اللَّهَ عَزِيزٌ أي: غالب لا يُعجزه عقابكم، حَكِيمٌ في إمهاله إلى وقت معلوم.\nالإشارة: أمر الحق جل جلاله جميع عباده بالصلح معه والاستسلام لأحكامه، بحيث لا يَصْدُر منهم نِزَاعٌ لأحكامه، ولا اعتراض على أفعاله، بل يَنظرون ما يبرز من عنصر القدرة، فيتلقونه بالرضى والتسليم، أو الصبر والتصبر، سواء ظهرت هذه الأفعال على أيدي الوسائط أو بلا وسائط، إذ لا فاعل سواه، وكلٍّ من عند الله، فإن\nزللتم واعترضتم، أو سخطتم، من بعد ما جاءتكم الآيات البينات الدالة على وحدانية الحق في ذاته وصفاته وأفعاله، فاعلموا أن الله عزيز حكيم، لا يعجزه عقوبتكم وإبعادكم، لكنه من حكمته يُمهل ولا يهمل، والله غالب على أمره، ومن تاب تاب الله عليه.\nثم ذكر وعيد من خالف أمره، فقال:","quran_ar":"يا أَيُّهَا الَّذِينَ آمَنُوا ادْخُلُوا فِي السِّلْمِ كَافَّةً وَلا تَتَّبِعُوا خُطُواتِ الشَّيْطانِ إِنَّهُ لَكُمْ عَدُوٌّ مُبِينٌ (208) فَإِنْ زَلَلْتُمْ مِنْ بَعْدِ ما جاءَتْكُمُ الْبَيِّناتُ فَاعْلَمُوا أَنَّ اللَّهَ عَزِيزٌ حَكِيمٌ (209)","quran_en":"208. You who believe, enter wholly into submission, and do not follow Satan’s footsteps. He is a manifest enemy to you. 209. If you slip after the clear proofs have come to you, know that God is Mighty, Wise.","segments":[{"type":"language","title":"Submission and completeness","html":"<p>I say: <i>salm<\/i> or <i>silm<\/i> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">السَّلْم، السِّلْم<\/bdi>), with either vowel, means surrender and compliance. Interpreting it here as a peace settlement is remote. <i>Kāffatan<\/i> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">كافة<\/bdi>), “wholly,” is a circumstantial expression (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">حال<\/bdi>) relating both to the subject pronoun and to “submission,” as in His saying, “She brought him to her people, carrying him” [19:27].<a class=\"source-marker\" href=\"Source_Reference_Ledger_XII.html#LEGACY-147\" title=\"Inherited reference occurrence; see current source status\">[B142]<\/a><\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"I say: salm or silm ( السَّلْم، السِّلْم ), with either vowel, means surrender and compliance. Interpreting it here as a peace settlement is remote. Kāffatan ( كافة ), “wholly,” is a circumstantial expression ( حال ) relating both to the subject pronoun and to “submission,” as in His saying, “She brought him to her people, carrying him” [19:27]. [B142]"},{"type":"exposition","title":"Entering all the ordinances of Islam","html":"<p>The Real, glorious is His majesty, says: you People of the Book who have believed in Muḥammad, God bless him and grant him peace, enter all the ordinances of Islam, neglecting none and turning to nothing else. It was revealed concerning ʿAbd Allāh ibn Salām and his companions, who entered Islam but wished to venerate the Sabbath and felt reluctance to eat camel meat. Or it concerns the hypocrites, who submitted outwardly but harboured hypocrisy inwardly. The Real tells them: you who believe outwardly, enter Islam altogether, outwardly and inwardly. Or it concerns Muslims, whom He commands to adhere to all Islam’s ordinances and investigate their rulings and mysteries.<\/p><p>Do not follow Satan’s footsteps: his paths leading to dividing and being divided. He is a manifest enemy to you, whose hostility is plain. If you slip from the straight road, dividing the parts of the Sacred Law or turning towards another law, after clear signs have come to you establishing the religion’s truth and Muḥammad’s prophethood, know that God is Mighty—prevailing, not unable to punish you—and Wise in granting respite until an appointed time.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real, glorious is His majesty, says: you People of the Book who have believed in Muḥammad, God bless him and grant him peace, enter all the ordinances of Islam, neglecting none and turning to nothing else. It was revealed concerning ʿAbd Allāh ibn Salām and his companions, who entered Islam but wished to venerate the Sabbath and felt reluctance to eat camel meat. Or it concerns the hypocrites, who submitted outwardly but harboured hypocrisy inwardly. The Real tells them: you who believe outwardly, enter Islam altogether, outwardly and inwardly. Or it concerns Muslims, whom He commands to adhere to all Islam’s ordinances and investigate their rulings and mysteries. Do not follow Satan’s footsteps: his paths leading to dividing and being divided. He is a manifest enemy to you, whose hostility is plain. If you slip from the straight road, dividing the parts of the Sacred Law or turning towards another law, after clear signs have come to you establishing the religion’s truth and Muḥammad’s prophethood, know that God is Mighty—prevailing, not unable to punish you—and Wise in granting respite until an appointed time."},{"type":"spiritual","title":"Making peace with God’s decrees","html":"<p>The Real commands all His servants to make peace with Him and surrender to His decrees, so that no contention with His rulings or objection to His acts issues from them. Rather, they look at what emerges from the source of power (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">عنصر القدرة<\/bdi>) and receive it with contentment and surrender (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الرضى والتسليم<\/bdi>), or patience and the effort to be patient (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الصبر والتصبر<\/bdi>), whether those acts appear through intermediaries or without intermediaries. There is no agent other than He; all is from God. If<\/p><p>you slip and object, or become displeased, after clear signs have come to you indicating the Real’s oneness in His Essence, attributes and acts, know that God is Mighty, Wise. Punishing and distancing you do not defeat Him; in His wisdom, however, He grants respite without neglecting. God prevails in His purpose. Whoever repents, God turns towards in mercy.<\/p>","authorial":true,"text":"The Real commands all His servants to make peace with Him and surrender to His decrees, so that no contention with His rulings or objection to His acts issues from them. Rather, they look at what emerges from the source of power ( عنصر القدرة ) and receive it with contentment and surrender ( الرضى والتسليم ), or patience and the effort to be patient ( الصبر والتصبر ), whether those acts appear through intermediaries or without intermediaries. There is no agent other than He; all is from God. If you slip and object, or become displeased, after clear signs have come to you indicating the Real’s oneness in His Essence, attributes and acts, know that God is Mighty, Wise. Punishing and distancing you do not defeat Him; in His wisdom, however, He grants respite without neglecting. God prevails in His purpose. Whoever repents, God turns towards in mercy."},{"type":"transition","title":"","html":"<p>He then mentions the threat against whoever opposes His command.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"He then mentions the threat against whoever opposes His command."}],"cairo_notes":[],"english_characters":2409,"arabic_characters":1914},{"id":"q2-210","q":"2:210","s":2,"surah":"Al-Baqara","title":"2:210 · The coming of God and the return of all affairs","source":"Cairo print · Volume I, pp. 236–237","cairo":"I:236–237","source_page_first":236,"source_page_last":237,"ar":"هَلْ يَنْظُرُونَ إِلاَّ أَنْ يَأْتِيَهُمُ اللَّهُ فِي ظُلَلٍ مِنَ الْغَمامِ وَالْمَلائِكَةُ وَقُضِيَ الْأَمْرُ وَإِلَى اللَّهِ تُرْجَعُ الْأُمُورُ (210)\nقلت: (الظُّلَل) : جمع ظُلَّه، وهي ما أظلَّك من فوق، و (الغَمام) : السحاب الرقيق الأبيض.\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: ما ينتظر هؤلاء الممتنعون من الدخول في شرائع الإسلام- إلا أن تقوم الساعة، ويأتيهم الله للفصل بين عباده فِي ظُلَلٍ مِنَ الْغَمامِ، بأن يتجلّى لعباده على ما يليق بجلاله إذ تجليات الحق لا تنحصر. وتأتيهم الْمَلائِكَةُ تحيط بهم وَقُضِيَ الْأَمْرُ بعذابهم، وَإِلَى اللَّهِ تُرْجَعُ الْأُمُورُ كلها، فهو المتصرف وحده. وقد ذكر المنذري حديث هذا التجلّي بطوله، وذكر فيه النزول والفصل بين عباده، والمرور على الصراط، والناس في أنوار إيمانهم. وذكره الفاسي في الحاشية بتمامه. ومن كَحل عين بصيرته بإثمد التوحيد الخاص(1)، لم يُسْتَصْعَبْ عليه فَهْمُ هذا الحديث وأمثاله لسعة دائرة معرفته. والله تعالى أعلم.\nالإشارة: في الآية تهديدٌ لأهل الحجاب الذين لم يتحققوا بالصلح مع الله، بل هم يخاصمون الله في مظاهر خلقه، ويعترضون على الله في قضائه وحكمه، فقال لهم الحق جلّ جلاله: هل ينتظر هؤلاء المنكرون عليَّ في أفعالي، المعترضون عليَّ في حكمي وإبرامي- إلا أن أتعرف لهم فِي ظُلَلٍ مِّنَ الْغَمَامِ، وهو سحب الآثار، فإذا أنكروني أخذتهم الملائكةُ، وقضي الأمر بهلاكهم، وإِلى الله تُرجع الأمورُ كلها، فليلتزم العبد الأدب مع مولاه، وليُسلم الأمور كلها إلى الله، إذ لا موجود سواه(2) ، فما برز من العباد: كله من الله، فمن اشتغل بعتابهم فاته الأدب مع الله، إلا ما أمرت به الشريعة، فليكن في ذلك كالعبد يُؤدب ابنَ سيده يده تؤدب وقلبه يعظم، والله تعالى أعلم وأرحم.\nثم هدّد بنى إسرائيل على عدم دخولهم فى الإسلام، أو على عدم تمسكهم بشرائعه كلها، فقال:","quran_ar":"هَلْ يَنْظُرُونَ إِلاَّ أَنْ يَأْتِيَهُمُ اللَّهُ فِي ظُلَلٍ مِنَ الْغَمامِ وَالْمَلائِكَةُ وَقُضِيَ الْأَمْرُ وَإِلَى اللَّهِ تُرْجَعُ الْأُمُورُ (210)","quran_en":"210. Are they waiting for anything but that God should come to them in canopies of cloud, and the angels, and the matter be decided? To God all affairs are returned.","segments":[{"type":"language","title":"Canopies and cloud","html":"<p>I say: <i>ẓulal<\/i> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">ظُلَل<\/bdi>) is the plural of <i>ẓulla<\/i>, whatever shades you from above. <i>Ghamām<\/i> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الغمام<\/bdi>) is fine, white cloud.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"I say: ẓulal ( ظُلَل ) is the plural of ẓulla , whatever shades you from above. Ghamām ( الغمام ) is fine, white cloud."},{"type":"theology","title":"Theophany and judgement","html":"<p>The Real, glorious is His majesty, says: those who refuse to enter Islam’s ordinances await only the Hour’s arising and God’s coming to decide between His servants in canopies of cloud. He discloses Himself to His servants in a manner befitting His majesty, for the Real’s self-disclosures (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">تجليات الحق<\/bdi>) are not confined. The angels come and surround them; the matter is decided through their punishment. To God all affairs return: He alone disposes of them.<\/p><p>Al-Mundhirī relates at length the Tradition concerning this self-disclosure, mentioning the descent, the judgement between His servants, and the crossing of the Bridge, with people in the lights of their faith. Al-Fāsī includes it in full in his gloss. One who has anointed the eye of inward sight with the antimony of the elect’s realization of unity (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">التوحيد الخاص<\/bdi>) will not find this Tradition and its like difficult to understand, because of the breadth of his gnosis’s compass. And God Most High knows best.<a class=\"source-marker\" href=\"Source_Reference_Ledger_XII.html#LEGACY-148\" title=\"Inherited reference occurrence; see current source status\">[B143]<\/a><\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real, glorious is His majesty, says: those who refuse to enter Islam’s ordinances await only the Hour’s arising and God’s coming to decide between His servants in canopies of cloud. He discloses Himself to His servants in a manner befitting His majesty, for the Real’s self-disclosures ( تجليات الحق ) are not confined. The angels come and surround them; the matter is decided through their punishment. To God all affairs return: He alone disposes of them. Al-Mundhirī relates at length the Tradition concerning this self-disclosure, mentioning the descent, the judgement between His servants, and the crossing of the Bridge, with people in the lights of their faith. Al-Fāsī includes it in full in his gloss. One who has anointed the eye of inward sight with the antimony of the elect’s realization of unity ( التوحيد الخاص ) will not find this Tradition and its like difficult to understand, because of the breadth of his gnosis’s compass. And God Most High knows best. [B143]"},{"type":"spiritual","title":"Recognizing the Real without abandoning the Law","html":"<p>The verse threatens the veiled who have not truly made peace with God, but dispute with Him in the manifestations of His creatures and object to His decree and judgement. The Real tells them: do those who deny Me in My acts and object to My ruling and determination await anything but My making Myself known to them in canopies of cloud—the clouds of created effects (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الآثار<\/bdi>)? When they deny Me, the angels seize them and their destruction is decided. All affairs return to God.<\/p><p>Let the servant therefore observe right comportment (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الأدب<\/bdi>) with his Master and entrust all affairs to God, for no existent is other than He. Whatever proceeds from servants is wholly from God. Whoever occupies himself with reproaching them misses right comportment with God—except in what the Sacred Law commands. There let him be like a servant disciplining his master’s son: his hand disciplines while his heart reveres. And God Most High knows best and is most merciful.<\/p>","authorial":true,"text":"The verse threatens the veiled who have not truly made peace with God, but dispute with Him in the manifestations of His creatures and object to His decree and judgement. The Real tells them: do those who deny Me in My acts and object to My ruling and determination await anything but My making Myself known to them in canopies of cloud—the clouds of created effects ( الآثار )? When they deny Me, the angels seize them and their destruction is decided. All affairs return to God. Let the servant therefore observe right comportment ( الأدب ) with his Master and entrust all affairs to God, for no existent is other than He. Whatever proceeds from servants is wholly from God. Whoever occupies himself with reproaching them misses right comportment with God—except in what the Sacred Law commands. There let him be like a servant disciplining his master’s son: his hand disciplines while his heart reveres. And God Most High knows best and is most merciful."},{"type":"transition","title":"","html":"<p>He then threatens the Children of Israel for not entering Islam, or for not adhering to all its ordinances.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"He then threatens the Children of Israel for not entering Islam, or for not adhering to all its ordinances."}],"cairo_notes":[{"id":"C236.1","page":236,"ar":"الإثمد: حجر يتخذ منه الكحل. وقيل: هو نفس الكحل.","en":"Ithmid: a stone from which kohl is made. It is also said to mean kohl itself.","anchor":"بإثمد التوحيد الخاص"},{"id":"C236.2","page":236,"ar":"أي: لا موجود بحق.","en":"That is: nothing exists in the true sense.","anchor":"إذ لا موجود سواه"}],"english_characters":2165,"arabic_characters":1573},{"id":"q2-211","q":"2:211","s":2,"surah":"Al-Baqara","title":"2:211 · Changing the blessing after receiving it","source":"Cairo print · Volume I, pp. 237–237","cairo":"I:237","source_page_first":237,"source_page_last":237,"ar":"سَلْ بَنِي إِسْرائِيلَ كَمْ آتَيْناهُمْ مِنْ آيَةٍ بَيِّنَةٍ وَمَنْ يُبَدِّلْ نِعْمَةَ اللَّهِ مِنْ بَعْدِ ما جاءَتْهُ فَإِنَّ اللَّهَ شَدِيدُ الْعِقابِ (211)\nقلت: (كم) خبرية، أو استفهامية، محلها نصب بفعل محذوف يُقدر مؤخراً للصدرية، أي: كم آياتنا آتيناهم، أو رفع بالابتداء، والعائد محذوف، أي: آتيناهموه.\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله لرسوله- عليه الصلاة والسلام- أو لكل سامع: سَلْ بَنِي إِسْرائِيلَ سؤال تقريع، وقل لهم: كَمْ آتَيْناهُمْ مِنْ آيَةٍ بَيِّنَةٍ أي: كثيراً ما آتيناهم من آية واضحة في شأنك، تدل على صدق رسالتك وعلو شأنك وفخامة أمرك، اعتناء بأمرهم، ونعمة على مَنْ أدرك زمانك منهم. ثم إنهم بدلوا نعمة الله كفرا، وجحدوا فكتموا تلك النعمة وكفروها، وَمَنْ يُبَدِّلْ نِعْمَةَ اللَّهِ من بعد مجيئها إياه، فَإِنَّ اللَّهَ شَدِيدُ الْعِقابِ لمن كفر نعمه وجحد رسله، نعوذ بالله من السلب بعد العطاء، ومن كفران النعم، وحرمان الرضا.\nالإشارة: ما قيل لبني إسرائيل، يقال لمن تحقق بولاية وليّ من أولياء الله، ثم جحدها وكتمها، وحرّم نفسه بركة ذلك الولي، فمات على مرضه، فيقال له: وَمَنْ يُبَدِّلْ نِعْمَةَ اللَّهِ مِنْ بَعْدِ ما جاءَتْهُ فَإِنَّ اللَّهَ شَدِيدُ الْعِقابِ. وعقوبته: أن يلقى الله بقلب سقيم، فيُبعث مع عوام أهل اليمين، ويُحرم درجة المقربين، التي تلي درجة النبيين والمرسلين. عائذاً بالله من الحرمان، وشُؤمِ عاقبةِ الخذلان.\nثم ذكر الحق جل جلاله سبب هذا الحرمان، وهو حب الدنيا، فقال:","quran_ar":"سَلْ بَنِي إِسْرائِيلَ كَمْ آتَيْناهُمْ مِنْ آيَةٍ بَيِّنَةٍ وَمَنْ يُبَدِّلْ نِعْمَةَ اللَّهِ مِنْ بَعْدِ ما جاءَتْهُ فَإِنَّ اللَّهَ شَدِيدُ الْعِقابِ (211)","quran_en":"211. Ask the Children of Israel how many a clear sign We gave them. Whoever changes God’s blessing after it has come to him—God is severe in punishment.","segments":[{"type":"language","title":"The construction of “how many”","html":"<p>I say: <i>kam<\/i> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">كم<\/bdi>) may be declarative or interrogative (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">خبرية أو استفهامية<\/bdi>). It occupies an accusative position through an omitted verb understood after it, because it must stand initially (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الصدرية<\/bdi>): “How many [We gave]—We gave them.” Or it is nominative as the subject (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الابتداء<\/bdi>), with the resumptive pronoun (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">العائد<\/bdi>) omitted: “We gave it to them.”<\/p><p><strong>Translator’s note:<\/strong> Cairo I:237 prints <bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">كم آياتنا آتيناهم<\/bdi>. The closely parallel grammatical discussion in Ibn ʿAṭiyya’s <i>al-Muḥarrar al-wajīz<\/i>, at 2:211, reads <bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">كم آتينا آتيناهم<\/bdi>. This makes explicit the supplied verb “We gave” required by the preceding explanation. The rendering follows that strongly supported reconstruction; the Cairo wording is recorded here, and no manuscript confirmation is claimed.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"I say: kam ( كم ) may be declarative or interrogative ( خبرية أو استفهامية ). It occupies an accusative position through an omitted verb understood after it, because it must stand initially ( الصدرية ): “How many [We gave]—We gave them.” Or it is nominative as the subject ( الابتداء ), with the resumptive pronoun ( العائد ) omitted: “We gave it to them.” Translator’s note: Cairo I:237 prints كم آياتنا آتيناهم . The closely parallel grammatical discussion in Ibn ʿAṭiyya’s al-Muḥarrar al-wajīz , at 2:211, reads كم آتينا آتيناهم . This makes explicit the supplied verb “We gave” required by the preceding explanation. The rendering follows that strongly supported reconstruction; the Cairo wording is recorded here, and no manuscript confirmation is claimed."},{"type":"exposition","title":"Signs received and then denied","html":"<p>The Real, glorious is His majesty, tells His Messenger—or every hearer—to ask the Children of Israel a question of rebuke: how many clear signs We gave them! That is, many a manifest sign concerning you, indicating your message’s truth, your exalted rank and the greatness of your affair, through care for them and as a blessing to those among them who lived in your time. Yet they exchanged God’s blessing for unbelief, denied it, concealed that blessing and were ungrateful for it. Whoever changes God’s blessing after receiving it—God is severe in punishing one who denies His blessings and rejects His messengers. We seek refuge in God from withdrawal after giving, ingratitude for blessings and deprivation of His approval.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real, glorious is His majesty, tells His Messenger—or every hearer—to ask the Children of Israel a question of rebuke: how many clear signs We gave them! That is, many a manifest sign concerning you, indicating your message’s truth, your exalted rank and the greatness of your affair, through care for them and as a blessing to those among them who lived in your time. Yet they exchanged God’s blessing for unbelief, denied it, concealed that blessing and were ungrateful for it. Whoever changes God’s blessing after receiving it—God is severe in punishing one who denies His blessings and rejects His messengers. We seek refuge in God from withdrawal after giving, ingratitude for blessings and deprivation of His approval."},{"type":"spiritual","title":"Depriving oneself of a saint’s blessing","html":"<p>What was said to the Children of Israel is said to one who recognizes the sainthood of one of God’s saints, then denies and conceals it, deprives himself of that saint’s blessing and dies in his sickness. He is told: whoever changes God’s blessing after it has come to him—God is severe in punishment. His punishment is to meet God with an ailing heart and be raised among the ordinary people of the right hand, deprived of the rank of the near ones that follows the rank of the prophets and messengers. We seek refuge in God from deprivation and the evil outcome of abandonment.<\/p>","authorial":true,"text":"What was said to the Children of Israel is said to one who recognizes the sainthood of one of God’s saints, then denies and conceals it, deprives himself of that saint’s blessing and dies in his sickness. He is told: whoever changes God’s blessing after it has come to him—God is severe in punishment. His punishment is to meet God with an ailing heart and be raised among the ordinary people of the right hand, deprived of the rank of the near ones that follows the rank of the prophets and messengers. We seek refuge in God from deprivation and the evil outcome of abandonment."},{"type":"transition","title":"","html":"<p>The Real then identifies the cause of this deprivation: love of the world.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real then identifies the cause of this deprivation: love of the world."}],"cairo_notes":[],"english_characters":2142,"arabic_characters":1275},{"id":"q2-212","q":"2:212","s":2,"surah":"Al-Baqara","title":"2:212 · Worldly adornment and the hidden works of the heart","source":"Cairo print · Volume I, pp. 237–238","cairo":"I:237–238","source_page_first":237,"source_page_last":238,"ar":"زُيِّنَ لِلَّذِينَ كَفَرُوا الْحَياةُ الدُّنْيا وَيَسْخَرُونَ مِنَ الَّذِينَ آمَنُوا وَالَّذِينَ اتَّقَوْا فَوْقَهُمْ يَوْمَ الْقِيامَةِ وَاللَّهُ يَرْزُقُ مَنْ يَشاءُ بِغَيْرِ حِسابٍ (212)\nقلت: (زُين) مبني للمفعول، والفاعل هو الله، إذ لا فاعل سواه.\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: زُيِّنَ لِلَّذِينَ كَفَرُوا من أهل الكتاب وغيرهم، الْحَياةُ الدُّنْيا أي: حُسِّنَتْ في أعينهم، وأُشربت محبتها في قلوبهم، حتى تهالكوا عليها، واعرضوا عن غيرها، فلم تتفرغ قلوبهم للتفكر والاعتبار، ولم تستمع آذانهم للوعظ والتذكار، بل أعمتهم، وأصمّتهم، وقصروا عليها همتهم، حتى جعلوا يسخرون ممن أعرض عنها، كفقراء المسلمين وأهل الصفة، فكانوا يستهزئون بهم، حيث رفضوا الدنيا وأقبلوا على الله، فرفعهم الله\nفي أعلى عليين، وخفض الكفار في أسفل سافلين. فهم يسخرون منهم في دار الدنيا وَالَّذِينَ اتَّقَوْا فَوْقَهُمْ يَوْمَ الْقِيامَةِ لأنهم في عليين، والآخرون في أسفل سافلين. أو لأنهم في كرامة، والآخرون في مذلة. أو لأنهم يسخرون منهم يوم القيامة كما سخروا منهم في الدنيا.\nوعبَّر بالتقوى لأنها سبب رفعهم واستعلائهم. وأما استهزاؤهم بهم لأجل فقرهم، فإن الفقر شرف للعبد، والبسط فى الدنيا لا يدل على شرفه فقد يكون استدراجاً، وقد يكون عوناً، فالله يَرْزُقُ مَنْ يَشاءُ بِغَيْرِ حِسابٍ، أي:\nبغير تقدير، فيوسع في الدنيا استدراجاً وابتلاء، ويقتر على من يشاء اختباراً وتمحيصاً، لاَ يُسْئَلُ عَمَّا يَفْعَلُ وَهُمْ يُسْئَلُونَ.\nالإشارة: اعلم أن عمل أهل الباطن كله باطني قلبي، بين تفكر واعتبار، وشهود واستبصار، أو نقول: بين فكرة ونظرة وعكوف في الحضرة، فلا يظهرون من أعمالهم إلا المهم من الواجبات، ولذلك قال بعضهم: إذا وصل العمل إلى القلوب استراحت الجوارح، (ومعلوم أن الذرة من أعمال القلوب أفضل من أمثال الجبال من أعمال الجوارح(1)) لأن أعمال القلوب خفية، لا يطلع عليها ملك فيكتبها، ولا شيطان فيفسدها، الإخلاص فيها محقق.\nوأيضاً: «تفكر ساعة أفضل من عبادة ستين سنة» . وسئل- عليه الصلاة والسلام-: «أيُّ الأَعْمَال أَفْضَلُ؟ قال:\nالعلْمُ بالله. قيلَ: يا رسُولَ الله سَأَلْنَاكَ عن العَمَل؟ فقال: العلمُ بالله، ثم قال صلّى الله عليه وسلم: إذا حَصَلَ العلمُ بالله كَفَى قلِيلُ العَملِ» . أو كما قال عليه الصلاة والسلام، فلما خفيت أعمال أهل الباطن سخر منهم أهل الظاهر، واستصغروا شأنهم حيث لم يروا عليهم من الأعمال ما رأوا على العُبَّاد والزُهاد. والذين اتقوا شهود ما سوى الله، أو كل ما يشغل عن الله، فوقهم يوم القيامة لأنهم من المقربين وغيرهم من عوام المسلمين، والله يرزق من يشاء في الدارين بغير حسابٍ، أي: بغير تقدير ولا حصر، فيرزق العلوم، ويفتح مخازن الفهوم على مَن توجه إلى مولاه، وفرغ قلبه مما سواه. وبالله التوفيق.\nثم ذكر الحق تعالى حكمة بعثه الرسل، فقال:","quran_ar":"زُيِّنَ لِلَّذِينَ كَفَرُوا الْحَياةُ الدُّنْيا وَيَسْخَرُونَ مِنَ الَّذِينَ آمَنُوا وَالَّذِينَ اتَّقَوْا فَوْقَهُمْ يَوْمَ الْقِيامَةِ وَاللَّهُ يَرْزُقُ مَنْ يَشاءُ بِغَيْرِ حِسابٍ (212)","quran_en":"212. Worldly life has been adorned for those who disbelieve, and they mock those who believe. Those who fear God will be above them on the Day of Resurrection. God provides for whom He wills without reckoning.","segments":[{"type":"language","title":"The unexpressed agent","html":"<p>I say: “Has been adorned” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">زُيّن<\/bdi>) is passive (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">مبني للمفعول<\/bdi>). Its agent is God, for there is no agent other than He.<\/p><p class=\"translator-note-ref\">[Translator’s print-source notes: <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/note/R07-08\">[R07-08]<\/a>]<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"I say: “Has been adorned” ( زُيّن ) is passive ( مبني للمفعول ). Its agent is God, for there is no agent other than He. [Translator’s print-source notes: [R07-08] ]"},{"type":"exposition","title":"The poor mocked in this world","html":"<p>The Real, glorious is His majesty, says: worldly life has been adorned for the disbelievers, whether People of the Book or others. It has been made beautiful in their eyes and its love made to permeate their hearts, until they throw themselves upon it and turn away from everything else. Their hearts find no leisure for reflection and taking heed; their ears do not listen to admonition and reminder. It blinds and deafens them, and they confine their aspiration to it, until they mock those who turn away from it, such as poor Muslims and the People of the Bench. They ridicule them for rejecting the world and turning towards God. God raises these<\/p><p>to the highest heights and lowers the disbelievers to the lowest depths. They mock them in this world, but those who fear God will be above them on the Day of Resurrection: they are in the heights, the others in the lowest depths; or they are honoured, the others abased; or they will mock the others on the Day of Resurrection as the others mocked them in this world.<\/p><p>He expresses their state by Godfearingness because it causes their elevation and superiority. As for being mocked for poverty, poverty is an honour to the servant. Worldly abundance does not prove his honour: it may be a gradual drawing on towards punishment (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">استدراج<\/bdi>), or it may be an aid. God provides for whom He wills without reckoning—that is, without prescribed measure. He gives worldly abundance as a gradual drawing on and a trial, and constricts provision for whom He wills as a test and purification. “He is not questioned about what He does, but they are questioned” [21:23].<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real, glorious is His majesty, says: worldly life has been adorned for the disbelievers, whether People of the Book or others. It has been made beautiful in their eyes and its love made to permeate their hearts, until they throw themselves upon it and turn away from everything else. Their hearts find no leisure for reflection and taking heed; their ears do not listen to admonition and reminder. It blinds and deafens them, and they confine their aspiration to it, until they mock those who turn away from it, such as poor Muslims and the People of the Bench. They ridicule them for rejecting the world and turning towards God. God raises these to the highest heights and lowers the disbelievers to the lowest depths. They mock them in this world, but those who fear God will be above them on the Day of Resurrection: they are in the heights, the others in the lowest depths; or they are honoured, the others abased; or they will mock the others on the Day of Resurrection as the others mocked them in this world. He expresses their state by Godfearingness because it causes their elevation and superiority. As for being mocked for poverty, poverty is an honour to the servant. Worldly abundance does not prove his honour: it may be a gradual drawing on towards punishment ( استدراج ), or it may be an aid. God provides for whom He wills without reckoning—that is, without prescribed measure. He gives worldly abundance as a gradual drawing on and a trial, and constricts provision for whom He wills as a test and purification. “He is not questioned about what He does, but they are questioned” [21:23]."},{"type":"spiritual","title":"Inward work and outward incomprehension","html":"<p>Know that the work of the inward folk (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">أهل الباطن<\/bdi>) is wholly inward and of the heart: reflection and taking heed, witnessing and inward perception—or, we may say, contemplation, vision and abiding in the Presence. They display of their works only the necessary obligations. Hence someone said: “When work reaches the hearts, the limbs rest.” It is known that a particle of the heart’s works is better than mountains of bodily works, because the heart’s works are hidden: no angel sees them to record them, and no devil sees them to spoil them. Sincerity is realized in them.<\/p><p>Moreover, “An hour’s reflection is better than sixty years’ worship.” The Prophet, blessings and peace be upon him, was asked: “Which works are best?” He replied: “Knowledge of God.” They said: “Messenger of God, we asked you about work.” He replied: “Knowledge of God,” and then said: “When knowledge of God is attained, little work suffices”—or as he said, blessings and peace be upon him.<a class=\"source-marker\" href=\"Source_Reference_Ledger_XII.html#LEGACY-149\" title=\"Inherited reference occurrence; see current source status\">[B144]<\/a><\/p><p>Because the inward folk’s works are hidden, the outward folk mock and belittle them, not seeing the works they see in worshippers and ascetics. Those who guard against witnessing anything other than God, or against everything that occupies them away from Him, will be above them on the Day of Resurrection: they are among the near ones, while the others are ordinary Muslims. God provides for whom He wills in both abodes without reckoning: without measure or restriction. He provides sciences and opens the treasuries of understanding to one who turns to his Master and empties his heart of everything else. And success is through God.<\/p>","authorial":true,"text":"Know that the work of the inward folk ( أهل الباطن ) is wholly inward and of the heart: reflection and taking heed, witnessing and inward perception—or, we may say, contemplation, vision and abiding in the Presence. They display of their works only the necessary obligations. Hence someone said: “When work reaches the hearts, the limbs rest.” It is known that a particle of the heart’s works is better than mountains of bodily works, because the heart’s works are hidden: no angel sees them to record them, and no devil sees them to spoil them. Sincerity is realized in them. Moreover, “An hour’s reflection is better than sixty years’ worship.” The Prophet, blessings and peace be upon him, was asked: “Which works are best?” He replied: “Knowledge of God.” They said: “Messenger of God, we asked you about work.” He replied: “Knowledge of God,” and then said: “When knowledge of God is attained, little work suffices”—or as he said, blessings and peace be upon him. [B144] Because the inward folk’s works are hidden, the outward folk mock and belittle them, not seeing the works they see in worshippers and ascetics. Those who guard against witnessing anything other than God, or against everything that occupies them away from Him, will be above them on the Day of Resurrection: they are among the near ones, while the others are ordinary Muslims. God provides for whom He wills in both abodes without reckoning: without measure or restriction. He provides sciences and opens the treasuries of understanding to one who turns to his Master and empties his heart of everything else. And success is through God."},{"type":"transition","title":"","html":"<p>The Real then mentions the wisdom of sending messengers.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real then mentions the wisdom of sending messengers."}],"cairo_notes":[{"id":"C238.1","page":238,"ar":"عزاه السراج الطوسي في اللمع إلى أبي سليمان الداراني. وقال السراج موضحا معناه: هذا الذي قال أبو سليمان يحتمل معنيين، أحدهما: أنه أراد بذلك: استراحت الجوارح من المجاهدات والمكابدات من الأعمال، إذا اشتغل بحفظ قلبه ومراعاة سره من الخواطر المشغلة والعوارض المذمومة التي تشغل قلبه عن ذكر الله تعالى. ويحتمل أيضا أنه أراد بذلك: أن يتمكن من المجاهدة، والأعمال والعبادات وتصير وطنه حتى يستلذها بقلبه ويجد حلاوتها، ويسقط عنه التعب ووجود الألم الذي كان يجد قبل ذلك.","en":"Al-Sarrāj al-Ṭūsī attributes it in al-Lumaʿ to Abū Sulaymān al-Dārānī. Explaining its meaning, al-Sarrāj says: What Abū Sulaymān said admits two meanings. First, he meant that the limbs rest from the disciplines and hardships of outward works when a person occupies himself with guarding his heart and watching over his inner consciousness against distracting thoughts and blameworthy intrusions that divert his heart from remembering God Most High. Alternatively, he meant that a person becomes established in spiritual discipline, works and acts of worship, and they become his familiar home, until his heart takes pleasure in them and tastes their sweetness, while the fatigue and experience of pain that he felt before fall away.","anchor":"ومعلوم أن الذرة من أعمال القلوب أفضل من أمثال الجبال من أعمال الجوارح"}],"english_characters":3441,"arabic_characters":2395},{"id":"q2-213","q":"2:213","s":2,"surah":"Al-Baqara","title":"2:213 · The one community, disagreement and prophetic guidance","source":"Cairo print · Volume I, pp. 239–240","cairo":"I:238–240","source_page_first":238,"source_page_last":240,"ar":"كانَ النَّاسُ أُمَّةً واحِدَةً فَبَعَثَ اللَّهُ النَّبِيِّينَ مُبَشِّرِينَ وَمُنْذِرِينَ وَأَنْزَلَ مَعَهُمُ الْكِتابَ بِالْحَقِّ\nلِيَحْكُمَ بَيْنَ النَّاسِ فِيمَا اخْتَلَفُوا فِيهِ وَمَا اخْتَلَفَ فِيهِ إِلاَّ الَّذِينَ أُوتُوهُ مِنْ بَعْدِ ما جاءَتْهُمُ الْبَيِّناتُ بَغْياً بَيْنَهُمْ فَهَدَى اللَّهُ الَّذِينَ آمَنُوا لِمَا اخْتَلَفُوا فِيهِ مِنَ الْحَقِّ بِإِذْنِهِ وَاللَّهُ يَهْدِي مَنْ يَشاءُ إِلى صِراطٍ مُسْتَقِيمٍ (213)\nقلت: (فبعث) معطوف على محذوف، أي: فاختلفوا فبعث، و (بغياً) : مفعول له، و (من الحق) بيان (لما) .\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: كانَ النَّاسُ في زمن آدم عليه السلام وما قرُب منه أُمَّةً واحِدَةً أي: جماعة واحدة، متفقة على التوحيد، والطاعة، فاختلفوا بعد ذلك في أمر التوحيد، فَبَعَثَ اللَّهُ النَّبِيِّينَ مُبَشِّرِينَ لأهل التوحيد والطاعة بالنعيم المقيم، وَمُنْذِرِينَ أي: مخوفين لأهل الكفر والعصيان بالعذاب الأليم.\nوَأَنْزَلَ مَعَهُمُ الْكِتابَ أي: جنس الكتب، فيشمل الكتب السماوية كلها، متلبساً ذلك الكتاب بِالْحَقِّ، ودالاً عليه لِيَحْكُمَ الحق تعالى على لسان الرسل بَيْنَ النَّاسِ في الأمر الذي اخْتَلَفُوا فِيهِ من أمر التوحيد وغيره. ثم اختلفوا أيضاً في الكتب المنزلة فبعضهم آمن، وبعضهم كفر بها أو ببعضها، وَمَا اخْتَلَفَ فِيهِ أي:\nفي الكتاب المنزل، إِلَّا الَّذِينَ أُوتُوهُ حسداً أو كبراً فاليهود آمنوا بالتوراة وكفروا بالإنجيل، والنصارى آمنوا بالإنجيل وكفروا بالتوراة، مِنْ بَعْدِ ما جاءَتْهُمُ: الآيات الواضحات في صحة ذلك الكتاب الذي كفروا به، والأمر بالإيمان به.\nوإنما وقع ذلك الكفر منهم بَغْياً وحسداً بَيْنَهُمْ، فأنزل الله العلم ليجمعهم ويؤلف بينهم على طاعته، فأمرهم أن يتألفوا بالعلم، فتحاسدوا، واختلفوا طلباً للرئاسة والجاه، فَهَدَى اللَّهُ الَّذِينَ آمَنُوا بمحمد- عليه الصلاة والسلام- للأمر الذي اختلف فيه أهل الكتاب، وهو الحق الذي جاءت به الرسل، فآمنوا بالجميع، وتآلفوا على طاعة الله بِإِذْنِهِ وإرادته، وَاللَّهُ يَهْدِي مَنْ يَشاءُ إِلى صِراطٍ مُسْتَقِيمٍ، ويضل من يشاء عن طريقه القويم، لاَ يُسْئَلُ عَمَّا يَفْعَلُ وَهُمْ يُسْئَلُونَ.\nالإشارة: الأصل في الأرواح كلها: الاتفاقُ والإقرار، وإنما حصل لها الخلاف والإنكار بعد دخولها في عالم الأشباح، وهبوطها من عالم الأرواح، فبعث الله النبيين يُذكْرون الناس العهدَ القديم، فمن سبقت له السعادة حصل له الإقرار، ومَن سبق له الشقاء حصل له الإنكار، ولذلك قال- عليه الصلاة والسلام-: «كُلُّ مَوْلُودٍ يُولَد على الفِطْرَةِ، فَأَبَوَاهُ يُهَوِّدَانِهِ أَوْ يُنَصّرَانِهِ أوْ يُمَجِّسَانِهِ» . ثم بعث الله الحكماء، وهم العارفون بالله، يعالجون ما حصل\nللروح من الجهل والإنكار، فمن سبقت له العناية آمن بهم، وصدقهم، واستسلم بكليته إليهم، فحصل له الوصول، وبلغ كل المأمول، ومن سبق له الحرمان لم يحصل له بهم إيمان، وبقي دائماً في قلبه حيران.\nوما وقع هذا الإنكار في الغالب إلا من أهل الرئاسة والجاه، أو من كان عبداً لدنياه وهواه، بغياً وحسداً منهم، فهدى الله الذين آمنوا- وهم أهل الفطرة والنيّة- لما اختلفوا فيه من الحق بإذنه، فحصل لهم التصديق، ووصلوا إلى عين التحقيق، وَاللَّهُ يَهْدِي مَنْ يَشاءُ إِلى صِراطٍ مُسْتَقِيمٍ وهو طريق الوصول إلى الحضرة القدسية التي كانت مقرّاً للأرواح الزكية، منها جاءت وإليها عادت. وفي ذلك يقول ابن البنا رضي الله عنه:\nوَهَذِهِ الحَقِيقَةُ النَّفْسِيَّةْ ... مَوْصُولةٌ بالحَضْرَةِ القُدْسِيَّهْ\nوَإِنَّمَا يَعْوقُهَا المَوْضُوعُ ... وَمِنْ هُنَا يُبْتَدأُ الطُّلُوعُ\nولمّا كانت المحبة والهداية إلى أسبابها مقرونتين بالبلاء ذكره الحق تعالى بإثر الهداية، فقال:","quran_ar":"كانَ النَّاسُ أُمَّةً واحِدَةً فَبَعَثَ اللَّهُ النَّبِيِّينَ مُبَشِّرِينَ وَمُنْذِرِينَ وَأَنْزَلَ مَعَهُمُ الْكِتابَ بِالْحَقِّ لِيَحْكُمَ بَيْنَ النَّاسِ فِيمَا اخْتَلَفُوا فِيهِ وَمَا اخْتَلَفَ فِيهِ إِلاَّ الَّذِينَ أُوتُوهُ مِنْ بَعْدِ ما جاءَتْهُمُ الْبَيِّناتُ بَغْياً بَيْنَهُمْ فَهَدَى اللَّهُ الَّذِينَ آمَنُوا لِمَا اخْتَلَفُوا فِيهِ مِنَ الْحَقِّ بِإِذْنِهِ وَاللَّهُ يَهْدِي مَنْ يَشاءُ إِلى صِراطٍ مُسْتَقِيمٍ (213)","quran_en":"213. People were one community. Then God sent the prophets as bearers of good news and warning, and sent down with them the Book in truth, to judge between people concerning what they disputed. None disputed it except those given it, after the clear proofs had come to them, out of mutual aggression. Then God guided those who believed, by His leave, to the truth about which they disputed. God guides whom He wills to a straight path.","segments":[{"type":"language","title":"An omitted disagreement and an expressed motive","html":"<p>I say: “Then He sent” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">فبعث<\/bdi>) is coordinated with something omitted (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">معطوف على محذوف<\/bdi>): “then they disagreed, so He sent.” “Out of aggression” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">بغياً<\/bdi>) is an accusative of purpose or cause (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">مفعول له<\/bdi>). “Of the truth” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">من الحق<\/bdi>) explains the relative “that about which” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">ما<\/bdi>).<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"I say: “Then He sent” ( فبعث ) is coordinated with something omitted ( معطوف على محذوف ): “then they disagreed, so He sent.” “Out of aggression” ( بغياً ) is an accusative of purpose or cause ( مفعول له ). “Of the truth” ( من الحق ) explains the relative “that about which” ( ما )."},{"type":"exposition","title":"Revelation intended to unite, and knowledge turned into rivalry","html":"<p>The Real, glorious is His majesty, says: in Adam’s time and the period near it, people were one community, one company agreeing upon unity and obedience. Afterwards they disagreed concerning divine unity. God then sent the prophets, bringing good news of abiding bliss to the people of unity and obedience, and warning the people of unbelief and disobedience of painful punishment.<\/p><p>He sent down with them the Book: the kind denoted by “books,” encompassing all heavenly scriptures. That Book was clothed in truth and indicated it, so that the Real, through the messengers’ tongues, might judge between people concerning their disagreements about divine unity and other matters. They then also disagreed concerning the revealed books: some believed, while others disbelieved in them or in part of them. None disagreed concerning the revealed Book except those given it, through envy or pride. Thus the Jews believed in the Torah and disbelieved in the Gospel, and the Christians believed in the Gospel and disbelieved in the Torah, after clear signs had come to them establishing the truth of the Book they rejected and commanding faith in it.<a class=\"source-marker\" href=\"Source_Reference_Ledger_XII.html#LEGACY-150\" title=\"Inherited reference occurrence; see current source status\">[B145]<\/a><\/p><p>This unbelief arose only through mutual aggression and envy. God sent down knowledge to gather and unite them in His obedience, commanding them to come together through knowledge; they envied one another and disagreed in pursuit of leadership and standing. God then guided those who believed in Muḥammad, blessings and peace be upon him, concerning what the People of the Book disputed: the truth the messengers brought. They believed in all of it and united in God’s obedience by His leave and will. God guides whom He wills to a straight path and leads whom He wills away from His upright way. “He is not questioned about what He does, but they are questioned” [21:23].<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real, glorious is His majesty, says: in Adam’s time and the period near it, people were one community, one company agreeing upon unity and obedience. Afterwards they disagreed concerning divine unity. God then sent the prophets, bringing good news of abiding bliss to the people of unity and obedience, and warning the people of unbelief and disobedience of painful punishment. He sent down with them the Book: the kind denoted by “books,” encompassing all heavenly scriptures. That Book was clothed in truth and indicated it, so that the Real, through the messengers’ tongues, might judge between people concerning their disagreements about divine unity and other matters. They then also disagreed concerning the revealed books: some believed, while others disbelieved in them or in part of them. None disagreed concerning the revealed Book except those given it, through envy or pride. Thus the Jews believed in the Torah and disbelieved in the Gospel, and the Christians believed in the Gospel and disbelieved in the Torah, after clear signs had come to them establishing the truth of the Book they rejected and commanding faith in it. [B145] This unbelief arose only through mutual aggression and envy. God sent down knowledge to gather and unite them in His obedience, commanding them to come together through knowledge; they envied one another and disagreed in pursuit of leadership and standing. God then guided those who believed in Muḥammad, blessings and peace be upon him, concerning what the People of the Book disputed: the truth the messengers brought. They believed in all of it and united in God’s obedience by His leave and will. God guides whom He wills to a straight path and leads whom He wills away from His upright way. “He is not questioned about what He does, but they are questioned” [21:23]."},{"type":"spiritual","title":"The spirits’ original acknowledgement and their return","html":"<p>The original state of all spirits is agreement and acknowledgement. Disagreement and denial arise only after their entry into the world of bodily forms and their descent from the world of spirits. God sent the prophets to remind people of the ancient covenant. Those for whom happiness had preceded acknowledged it; those for whom wretchedness had preceded denied it. Hence the Prophet said: “Every newborn is born upon the original disposition (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الفطرة<\/bdi>); its parents make it a Jew, a Christian or a Magian.”<\/p><p>God then sent the sages, the gnostics of God, to treat the ignorance and denial that had overtaken<\/p><p>the spirit. Those for whom divine care preceded believed in them, affirmed them and surrendered wholly to them. They attained arrival and reached all they hoped for. Those for whom deprivation preceded did not attain faith in them and remained always bewildered in their hearts.<\/p><p>This denial generally comes only from people of leadership and standing, or those enslaved to their world and desire, through their aggression and envy. God guided those who believed—the people of original disposition and intention—to the truth about which others disagreed, by His leave. They attained affirmation and reached realization itself. God guides whom He wills to a straight path: the path of arrival at the Holy Presence, which was the dwelling of pure spirits. From it they came, and to it they returned. Concerning this Ibn al-Bannā, God be pleased with him, says:<\/p><blockquote class=\"poetry\">This reality of the soul is joined to the Holy Presence;<br>only its bodily substrate impedes it,<br>and from here the ascent begins.<a class=\"source-marker\" href=\"Source_Reference_Ledger_XII.html#LEGACY-151\" title=\"Inherited reference occurrence; see current source status\">[B146]<\/a><\/blockquote>","authorial":true,"text":"The original state of all spirits is agreement and acknowledgement. Disagreement and denial arise only after their entry into the world of bodily forms and their descent from the world of spirits. God sent the prophets to remind people of the ancient covenant. Those for whom happiness had preceded acknowledged it; those for whom wretchedness had preceded denied it. Hence the Prophet said: “Every newborn is born upon the original disposition ( الفطرة ); its parents make it a Jew, a Christian or a Magian.” God then sent the sages, the gnostics of God, to treat the ignorance and denial that had overtaken the spirit. Those for whom divine care preceded believed in them, affirmed them and surrendered wholly to them. They attained arrival and reached all they hoped for. Those for whom deprivation preceded did not attain faith in them and remained always bewildered in their hearts. This denial generally comes only from people of leadership and standing, or those enslaved to their world and desire, through their aggression and envy. God guided those who believed—the people of original disposition and intention—to the truth about which others disagreed, by His leave. They attained affirmation and reached realization itself. God guides whom He wills to a straight path: the path of arrival at the Holy Presence, which was the dwelling of pure spirits. From it they came, and to it they returned. Concerning this Ibn al-Bannā, God be pleased with him, says: This reality of the soul is joined to the Holy Presence; only its bodily substrate impedes it, and from here the ascent begins. [B146]"},{"type":"transition","title":"","html":"<p>Since love and guidance to its means are joined to trial, the Real mentions trial immediately after guidance.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"Since love and guidance to its means are joined to trial, the Real mentions trial immediately after guidance."}],"cairo_notes":[],"english_characters":3812,"arabic_characters":3156},{"id":"q2-214","q":"2:214","s":2,"surah":"Al-Baqara","title":"2:214 · Trial, trembling and the nearness of help","source":"Cairo print · Volume I, pp. 240–241","cairo":"I:240–241","source_page_first":240,"source_page_last":241,"ar":"أَمْ حَسِبْتُمْ أَنْ تَدْخُلُوا الْجَنَّةَ وَلَمَّا يَأْتِكُمْ مَثَلُ الَّذِينَ خَلَوْا مِنْ قَبْلِكُمْ مَسَّتْهُمُ الْبَأْساءُ وَالضَّرَّاءُ وَزُلْزِلُوا حَتَّى يَقُولُ الرَّسُولُ وَالَّذِينَ آمَنُوا مَعَهُ مَتى نَصْرُ اللَّهِ أَلا إِنَّ نَصْرَ اللَّهِ قَرِيبٌ (214)\nقلت: «أم» منقطعة بمعنى بل، وتتضمن استفهاماً إنكاريّاً، و «حسب» تتعدى إلى مفعولين، أي: أظننتم دخول الجنة حاصلاً من غير أن يأتيكم؟. (لما) أصلها (لم) زيدت عليها «ما» : وهي تدل على توقع منفيها بخلاف لم.\nو (حتى يقول) يصح فيه النصب بتقدير (أن) لأن الزلزلة متقدمة على قول الرسول، والرفع على حكاية الحال، أي: وزلزلوا حتى حالتهم حينئذٍ أن الرسول ومن معه يقولون كذا وكذا. وفائدة الحكاية: فرض ما كان واقعاً في الزمان الماضي واقعاً في هذا الزمان، تصوّراً لتلك الحال العجيبة، واستحضاراً لصورتها في مشاهدة السامع، وإنما وجب رفعه عند إرادة الحال لأن نصبه يؤدي إلى تقدير (أن) ، وهي للاستقبال، والحال يُنافيه، ويصح في موضع «حتى» الداخلة على الحال الفاء السببية.\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله للرسول- عليه الصلاة والسلام- والمؤمنين، تسلية لهم وتشجيعاً لقلوبهم: أظننتم أَن تَدْخُلُواْ الْجَنَّةَ ولمَّا يُصبكم مثلُ ما أصاب مَنْ قبلَكم من الأنبياء وأممهم، فقد مَسَّتْهُمُ الْبَأْساءُ في أموالهم بالغصب والنهب والموت وَالضَّرَّاءُ فى أبدانهم بالقتل في الحرب والمرض وأنواع البلاء، وَزُلْزِلُوا أي:\nضُربوا بالمحن والشدائد، وطال عليهم البلاء، وتأخر عنهم النصر، حتى أفضى بهم الحال إلى أن قالوا: مَتى يأتينا نَصْرُ اللَّهِ؟ استبطاء لمجيئه مع شدة البلاء.\nقال الحق جل جلاله بشارةً لهم: أَلا إِنَّ نَصْرَ اللَّهِ قَرِيبٌ فلا تستعجلوا، وَاصْبِرُوا إِنَّ اللَّهَ مَعَ الصَّابِرِينَ وَالْعاقِبَةُ لِلْمُتَّقِينَ.\nالإشارة: الجنة حفت بالمكاره، ولا فرق بين جنة الزخارف وجنة المعارف، فمن رام دخول جنة المعارف قبل أن يمسه شيء من المكاره، فقد رام المحال. قال أبو المواهب: من ادّعى شهود الجمال، قبل تأدُّبه بالجلال، فارفضه فإنه دجال. وقال بعض العارفين: [صيحة العدو سوط الله يزجر به قلوب أوليائه لئلا تسكن إلى غيره] . وفي الحِكَم: «إنما أجرى الأذى عليهم كي لا تكون ساكناً إليهم، أراد أن يُزْعِجَكَ عن كل شيء حتى لا تكون ساكناً إلى شيء» . وقال الشيخ أبو الحسن رضي الله عنه: [اللهم إنَّ القوم قد حكمت عليهم بالذل حتى عزُّوا، وحكمت عليهم بالفقْدِ حتى وَجَدُوا] . فتسليط الخلق على أولياء الله في بدايتهم سنة ماضية، وحكمة إلهية، وَلَنْ تَجِدَ لِسُنَّةِ اللَّهِ تَبْدِيلًا.\nحتى إذا تخلصوا من البقايا، وكملت فيهم المزايا، نشر فضيلتهم لعباده، فأقروهم ليُعرفُوهم الطريق إلى الله، ويدلوا العباد على الله، بعد أن كساهم حينئذٍ كُسوة الجمال وكسوة الجلال، فبكسوة الجمال يقع الائتلاف عليهم والعطف لهم، وبكسوة الجلال يقع الامتثال لأمرهم والاستماع لقولهم. والله تعالى أعلم.\nولما أمر الحق تعالى بالنفقة في الجهاد وغيره، سألوا ما الذي ينفقون؟، فبيَّن الله تعالى لهم المنفَق والمحل الذي تُدفع فيه، فقال:","quran_ar":"أَمْ حَسِبْتُمْ أَنْ تَدْخُلُوا الْجَنَّةَ وَلَمَّا يَأْتِكُمْ مَثَلُ الَّذِينَ خَلَوْا مِنْ قَبْلِكُمْ مَسَّتْهُمُ الْبَأْساءُ وَالضَّرَّاءُ وَزُلْزِلُوا حَتَّى يَقُولُ الرَّسُولُ وَالَّذِينَ آمَنُوا مَعَهُ مَتى نَصْرُ اللَّهِ أَلا إِنَّ نَصْرَ اللَّهِ قَرِيبٌ (214)","quran_en":"214. Or did you think you would enter the Garden before there came to you the like of what befell those who passed before you? Hardship and distress touched them, and they were shaken until the messenger and those who believed with him said, “When will God’s help come?” Indeed, God’s help is near.","segments":[{"type":"language","title":"Expectation and the vivid presentation of a past scene","html":"<p>I say: <i>am<\/i> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">أم<\/bdi>) is discontinuous (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">منقطعة<\/bdi>), meaning “rather,” and includes a reproachful question (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">استفهام إنكاري<\/bdi>). <i>Ḥasiba<\/i>, “to suppose,” takes two objects: “Did you suppose entry into the Garden to be attainable without this coming upon you?” <i>Lammā<\/i> originally consists of <i>lam<\/i> with <i>mā<\/i> added. Unlike <i>lam<\/i>, it implies expectation of what it negates.<\/p><p>“Until the messenger says” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">حتى يقول<\/bdi>) permits the subjunctive (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">النصب<\/bdi>) through an understood <i>an<\/i>, because the shaking precedes the messenger’s speech. It also permits the indicative (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الرفع<\/bdi>), representing the situation as present (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">حكاية الحال<\/bdi>): they were shaken until their condition then was that the messenger and those with him were saying such and such. The benefit of this representation is to present what occurred in past time as occurring now, depicting that astonishing situation and bringing its image before the hearer’s witnessing. The indicative is required when a present situation is intended, because the subjunctive would imply <i>an<\/i>, which points forward, whereas present occurrence is incompatible with that. The causal <i>fāʾ<\/i> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الفاء السببية<\/bdi>) can stand where <i>ḥattā<\/i> introduces this present situation.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"I say: am ( أم ) is discontinuous ( منقطعة ), meaning “rather,” and includes a reproachful question ( استفهام إنكاري ). Ḥasiba , “to suppose,” takes two objects: “Did you suppose entry into the Garden to be attainable without this coming upon you?” Lammā originally consists of lam with mā added. Unlike lam , it implies expectation of what it negates. “Until the messenger says” ( حتى يقول ) permits the subjunctive ( النصب ) through an understood an , because the shaking precedes the messenger’s speech. It also permits the indicative ( الرفع ), representing the situation as present ( حكاية الحال ): they were shaken until their condition then was that the messenger and those with him were saying such and such. The benefit of this representation is to present what occurred in past time as occurring now, depicting that astonishing situation and bringing its image before the hearer’s witnessing. The indicative is required when a present situation is intended, because the subjunctive would imply an , which points forward, whereas present occurrence is incompatible with that. The causal fāʾ ( الفاء السببية ) can stand where ḥattā introduces this present situation."},{"type":"exposition","title":"The trials of earlier believers","html":"<p>The Real, glorious is His majesty, says to the Messenger and believers, consoling and strengthening their hearts: did you suppose that you would enter the Garden before the like of what befell earlier prophets and their communities had befallen you? Hardship touched their wealth through seizure, pillage and loss; distress touched their bodies through killing in war, illness and varieties of affliction. They were shaken: struck by trials and hardships. Their affliction lasted, and help was delayed, until their condition brought them to say, “When will God’s help come to us?” They found its coming slow in the intensity of their trial.<\/p><p>The Real gives them good news: indeed, God’s help is near. Do not be hasty; “be patient: God is with the patient” [8:46], and “the final outcome belongs to the Godfearing” [7:128].<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real, glorious is His majesty, says to the Messenger and believers, consoling and strengthening their hearts: did you suppose that you would enter the Garden before the like of what befell earlier prophets and their communities had befallen you? Hardship touched their wealth through seizure, pillage and loss; distress touched their bodies through killing in war, illness and varieties of affliction. They were shaken: struck by trials and hardships. Their affliction lasted, and help was delayed, until their condition brought them to say, “When will God’s help come to us?” They found its coming slow in the intensity of their trial. The Real gives them good news: indeed, God’s help is near. Do not be hasty; “be patient: God is with the patient” [8:46], and “the final outcome belongs to the Godfearing” [7:128]."},{"type":"spiritual","title":"Beauty reached through discipline under Majesty","html":"<p>The Garden is surrounded by hardships. There is no difference in this between the Garden of adornments and the Garden of gnoses (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">جنة الزخارف، جنة المعارف<\/bdi>). Whoever seeks entry into the Garden of gnoses before any hardship touches him seeks the impossible. Abū al-Mawāhib said: “Reject anyone who claims to witness Beauty before being disciplined by Majesty: he is an impostor.” A gnostic said: “The enemy’s cry is God’s whip, driving His saints’ hearts away from repose in anything other than Him.”<\/p><p>The <i>Ḥikam<\/i> says: “He made injury reach you through them only so that you would not repose in them. He wished to dislodge you from everything, so that you would repose in nothing.” <em>[Translator’s note: Cairo I, p. 241, reads <bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">عليهم<\/bdi> (ʿalayhim, “upon them”). Ibn ʿAjība’s own <i>Īqāẓ al-himam<\/i> quotes the same construction and then explicitly explains that God makes the injury reach you <bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">على أيدي الخلق إليك<\/bdi>, “through other people’s hands” (Dār al-Maʿārif, p. 488). The English follows this authorial explanation of the intended agency; the collated Arabic retains Cairo’s wording. The second sentence also retains Cairo’s distinct ending, “repose in nothing,” rather than the <i>Īqāẓ<\/i>/<i>Ḥikam<\/i> ending, “nothing distracts you from Him.”]<\/em> Shaykh Abū al-Ḥasan, God be pleased with him, said: “O God, You decreed abasement upon the folk until they became mighty, and loss upon them until they found.”<a class=\"source-marker\" href=\"Source_Reference_Ledger_XII.html#LEGACY-152\" title=\"Inherited reference occurrence; see current source status\">[B147]<\/a><\/p><p>Allowing creatures power over God’s saints at their beginnings is an established divine way and wisdom: “You will find no change in God’s way” [33:62]. When they are freed of the remnants and their excellences are completed, He spreads their merit among His servants. People acknowledge them so that they may make known the path to God and direct servants towards Him. By then He has clothed them in the garment of Beauty and the garment of Majesty. Through Beauty’s garment people come together around them and incline affectionately towards them; through Majesty’s garment people obey their commands and listen to their words. And God Most High knows best.<\/p>","authorial":true,"text":"The Garden is surrounded by hardships. There is no difference in this between the Garden of adornments and the Garden of gnoses ( جنة الزخارف، جنة المعارف ). Whoever seeks entry into the Garden of gnoses before any hardship touches him seeks the impossible. Abū al-Mawāhib said: “Reject anyone who claims to witness Beauty before being disciplined by Majesty: he is an impostor.” A gnostic said: “The enemy’s cry is God’s whip, driving His saints’ hearts away from repose in anything other than Him.” The Ḥikam says: “He made injury reach you through them only so that you would not repose in them. He wished to dislodge you from everything, so that you would repose in nothing.” [Translator’s note: Cairo I, p. 241, reads عليهم (ʿalayhim, “upon them”). Ibn ʿAjība’s own Īqāẓ al-himam quotes the same construction and then explicitly explains that God makes the injury reach you على أيدي الخلق إليك , “through other people’s hands” (Dār al-Maʿārif, p. 488). The English follows this authorial explanation of the intended agency; the collated Arabic retains Cairo’s wording. The second sentence also retains Cairo’s distinct ending, “repose in nothing,” rather than the Īqāẓ / Ḥikam ending, “nothing distracts you from Him.”] Shaykh Abū al-Ḥasan, God be pleased with him, said: “O God, You decreed abasement upon the folk until they became mighty, and loss upon them until they found.” [B147] Allowing creatures power over God’s saints at their beginnings is an established divine way and wisdom: “You will find no change in God’s way” [33:62]. When they are freed of the remnants and their excellences are completed, He spreads their merit among His servants. People acknowledge them so that they may make known the path to God and direct servants towards Him. By then He has clothed them in the garment of Beauty and the garment of Majesty. Through Beauty’s garment people come together around them and incline affectionately towards them; through Majesty’s garment people obey their commands and listen to their words. And God Most High knows best."},{"type":"transition","title":"","html":"<p>When the Real commanded expenditure in fighting in His path and elsewhere, they asked what they should spend. God explained what should be spent and where it should be given.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"When the Real commanded expenditure in fighting in His path and elsewhere, they asked what they should spend. God explained what should be spent and where it should be given."}],"cairo_notes":[],"english_characters":4219,"arabic_characters":2601},{"id":"q2-215","q":"2:215","s":2,"surah":"Al-Baqara","title":"2:215 · Where to spend wealth and where to offer the self","source":"Cairo print · Volume I, pp. 241–242","cairo":"I:241–242","source_page_first":241,"source_page_last":242,"ar":"يَسْئَلُونَكَ ماذا يُنْفِقُونَ قُلْ ما أَنْفَقْتُمْ مِنْ خَيْرٍ فَلِلْوالِدَيْنِ وَالْأَقْرَبِينَ وَالْيَتامى وَالْمَساكِينِ وَابْنِ السَّبِيلِ وَما تَفْعَلُوا مِنْ خَيْرٍ فَإِنَّ اللَّهَ بِهِ عَلِيمٌ (215)\nقلت: (ماذا) إما مفعول (يُنفقون) ، أو مبتدأ وخبر بحذف العائد، أي: ما الذي ينفقونه، والسائل هو عمرو بن الجُموح، كان ذا مال فقال: يا رسول الله، ماذا ننفق من أموالنا، وأين نضعها؟ فنزلت الآية.\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: يَسْئَلُونَكَ يا محمد ماذا يُنْفِقُونَ من أموالهم؟ قُلْ لهم: ما أَنْفَقْتُمْ مِنْ خَيْرٍ أيّ خير كان، ذهباً أو فضة أو طعاماً أو ثياباً أو حيواناً أو غير ذلك، فادفعوه للأهمّ فالأهم كالوالدين والأقربين لأن فيهم الصلة والصدقة، وَالْيَتامى الذين مات آباؤهم لهضم حالهم، وَالْمَساكِينِ لضعفهم، وَابْنِ السَّبِيلِ لغربته واحتياجه إلى ما يُبلِّغه إلى وطنه، وَما تَفْعَلُوا مِنْ خَيْرٍ يجازيكم به الله، فلا يخفى عليه شيء من أحوالكم، وهذه النفقة غير الزكاة، فلا نسخ فى الآية. والله تعالى أعلم.\nالإشارة: الإنفاق على قسمين: حسىّ ومعنوى، الإنفاق الحسيّ هو بذل الأموال والفلوس، والإنفاق المعنوي هو بذل الأرواح والنفوس، فمن بذل أمواله لله عوضه الله جنة الزخارف، ومن بذل نفسه لله عوضه الله جنة المعارف، ومن دخل جنة المعارف لا يشتاق إلى جنة الزخارف، وكما أن لنفقة الأموال محلا تصرف فيه، كما ذكره الحقّ تعالى هنا، كذلك لنفقة النفوس محل تصرف فيه وهو خدمة الشيوخ العارفين بالله، والإخوان الذين يستعين بهم على الوصول إلى الله، وكذلك من احتاج إليه من اليتامى الذين لا شيخ لهم، فيرشدهم وينصحهم، والمساكين الضعفاء الذين لا قدرة لهم على مجاهدة نفوسهم، فيقويهم بحاله أو مقاله، والغريب الذي انفرد عن الإخوان، ولم يجد ما يستعين به على سيره فيرشده إلى الصحبة والاجتماع بأهل المحبة، وإلى هذا المنزع أشار الشيخ أبو مدين رضي الله عنه:\nوَبالتغنِّي عَلى الإخوَانِ جُدْ أبَدا ... حِسّاً ومعنىً، وغُضَّ الطَّرْفَ إنْ عَثَرَا\nولمَّا ذكر الحق جلّ جلاله قواعد الإسلام، وهي الصلاةُ والزكاة والصوم والحج، بعد أن أشار إلى كلمة التوحيد بقوله: وَإِلهُكُمْ إِلهٌ واحِدٌ، ذكر الجهاد- الذي هو حفظ نظامه- فقال:","quran_ar":"يَسْئَلُونَكَ ماذا يُنْفِقُونَ قُلْ ما أَنْفَقْتُمْ مِنْ خَيْرٍ فَلِلْوالِدَيْنِ وَالْأَقْرَبِينَ وَالْيَتامى وَالْمَساكِينِ وَابْنِ السَّبِيلِ وَما تَفْعَلُوا مِنْ خَيْرٍ فَإِنَّ اللَّهَ بِهِ عَلِيمٌ (215)","quran_en":"215. They ask you what they should spend. Say, “Whatever good you spend is for parents, close relatives, orphans, the needy and the traveller.” Whatever good you do, God knows it.","segments":[{"type":"language","title":"What to give, and to whom","html":"<p>I say: <i>mādhā<\/i> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">ماذا<\/bdi>) may be the object of “spend,” or a subject and predicate (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">مبتدأ وخبر<\/bdi>) with the resumptive pronoun omitted: “What is it that they should spend?” The questioner was ʿAmr ibn al-Jamūḥ, a wealthy man, who asked: “Messenger of God, what should we spend from our wealth, and where should we place it?” The verse was revealed.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"I say: mādhā ( ماذا ) may be the object of “spend,” or a subject and predicate ( مبتدأ وخبر ) with the resumptive pronoun omitted: “What is it that they should spend?” The questioner was ʿAmr ibn al-Jamūḥ, a wealthy man, who asked: “Messenger of God, what should we spend from our wealth, and where should we place it?” The verse was revealed."},{"type":"exposition","title":"Priority among recipients","html":"<p>The Real, glorious is His majesty, says: they ask you, Muḥammad, what they should spend from their wealth. Tell them: whatever good you spend—gold, silver, food, clothing, livestock or anything else—give it in order of greatest need and priority. Parents and close relatives come first, because giving to them combines maintaining kinship with charity; then orphans whose fathers have died, because their condition is diminished; the needy, because of their weakness; and the traveller, because he is away from home and needs what will enable him to return. Whatever good you do, God will recompense you for it: none of your conditions is hidden from Him. This expenditure is other than the obligatory alms (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الزكاة<\/bdi>); thus the verse is not abrogated. And God Most High knows best.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real, glorious is His majesty, says: they ask you, Muḥammad, what they should spend from their wealth. Tell them: whatever good you spend—gold, silver, food, clothing, livestock or anything else—give it in order of greatest need and priority. Parents and close relatives come first, because giving to them combines maintaining kinship with charity; then orphans whose fathers have died, because their condition is diminished; the needy, because of their weakness; and the traveller, because he is away from home and needs what will enable him to return. Whatever good you do, God will recompense you for it: none of your conditions is hidden from Him. This expenditure is other than the obligatory alms ( الزكاة ); thus the verse is not abrogated. And God Most High knows best."},{"type":"spiritual","title":"Expenditure in sense and meaning","html":"<p>Expenditure is of two kinds: sensible and inward (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">حسي ومعنوي<\/bdi>). Sensible expenditure is giving wealth and money; inward expenditure is giving spirits and selves. Whoever gives his wealth for God receives in exchange the Garden of adornments; whoever gives himself for God receives in exchange the Garden of gnoses. One who enters the Garden of gnoses does not yearn for the Garden of adornments.<\/p><p>Just as expenditure of wealth has a place to be directed, as the Real mentions here, expenditure of selves has its place: serving shaykhs who know God, and brethren through whom one seeks aid in arriving at God. It also includes those who need one: orphans with no shaykh, whom one guides and counsels; the weak and needy who cannot struggle against their lower selves, whom one strengthens by one’s state or speech; and the stranger separated from the brethren, who finds nothing to assist his journey. One guides him towards companionship and gathering with the people of love. Shaykh Abū Madyan, God be pleased with him, points to this meaning:<\/p><blockquote class=\"poetry\">Be ever generous to the brethren, needing nothing from them,<br>in sense and meaning; lower your gaze when one stumbles.<a class=\"source-marker\" href=\"Source_Reference_Ledger_XII.html#LEGACY-153\" title=\"Inherited reference occurrence; see current source status\">[B148]<\/a><\/blockquote>","authorial":true,"text":"Expenditure is of two kinds: sensible and inward ( حسي ومعنوي ). Sensible expenditure is giving wealth and money; inward expenditure is giving spirits and selves. Whoever gives his wealth for God receives in exchange the Garden of adornments; whoever gives himself for God receives in exchange the Garden of gnoses. One who enters the Garden of gnoses does not yearn for the Garden of adornments. Just as expenditure of wealth has a place to be directed, as the Real mentions here, expenditure of selves has its place: serving shaykhs who know God, and brethren through whom one seeks aid in arriving at God. It also includes those who need one: orphans with no shaykh, whom one guides and counsels; the weak and needy who cannot struggle against their lower selves, whom one strengthens by one’s state or speech; and the stranger separated from the brethren, who finds nothing to assist his journey. One guides him towards companionship and gathering with the people of love. Shaykh Abū Madyan, God be pleased with him, points to this meaning: Be ever generous to the brethren, needing nothing from them, in sense and meaning; lower your gaze when one stumbles. [B148]"},{"type":"transition","title":"","html":"<p>Having mentioned Islam’s pillars—prayer, obligatory alms, fasting and pilgrimage—after pointing to the declaration of unity with “Your God is One God,” the Real mentions fighting in His path, which preserves its order.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"Having mentioned Islam’s pillars—prayer, obligatory alms, fasting and pilgrimage—after pointing to the declaration of unity with “Your God is One God,” the Real mentions fighting in His path, which preserves its order."}],"cairo_notes":[],"english_characters":2511,"arabic_characters":1878},{"id":"q2-216","q":"2:216","s":2,"surah":"Al-Baqara","title":"2:216 · What the self dislikes and what God knows to be good","source":"Cairo print · Volume I, pp. 242–243","cairo":"I:242–243","source_page_first":242,"source_page_last":243,"ar":"كُتِبَ عَلَيْكُمُ الْقِتالُ وَهُوَ كُرْهٌ لَكُمْ وَعَسى أَنْ تَكْرَهُوا شَيْئاً وَهُوَ خَيْرٌ لَكُمْ وَعَسى أَنْ تُحِبُّوا شَيْئاً وَهُوَ شَرٌّ لَكُمْ وَاللَّهُ يَعْلَمُ وَأَنْتُمْ لا تَعْلَمُونَ (216)\nقلت: الكره- بالضم-: اسم لما يَشُقُّ على النفس، وبالفتح المَصْدَر.\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: فُرض عليكم الجهاد، وهو شاق عليكم، تكرهه نفوسكم، وفيه خير كبير لكم، وَعَسى أَنْ تَكْرَهُوا شَيْئاً وَهُوَ خَيْرٌ لَكُمْ، ففي الجهاد نصر دينكم، وإعلاء كلمة إسلامكم، والغنيمة والظّفْرُ بعدوكم، والأجر الكبير عند ربكم، من مات كان شهيداً، ومن عاش عاش سعيداً، وكذلك بقية التكاليف، فإن النفس تكره الإقدام عليها، وهي مَنَاط صلاحها، وسبب فلاحها، وَعَسى أَنْ تُحِبُّوا شَيْئاً وَهُوَ شَرٌّ لَكُمْ فقد تحبون الراحة وترك الجهاد وفي ذلك ذُلُّكُمْ، وظهورُ العدو عليكم، وفوات الأجر من ربكم، وحرمان درجة الشهادة عند ربكم. وكذلك جميع المنهيات فإن النفس تحبها بالطبع، وتَشْرَهُ إليها، وهي تُفضي بها إلى ذلها وهوانها، وعبَّر الحقّ سبحانه بعسى لأن النفس إذا ارتاضت انعكس الأمر عليها، فيخف عليها أمر الطاعة، ويصعب عليها أمر المخالفة، وَاللَّهُ يَعْلَمُ ما فيه مصلحتكم، وَأَنْتُمْ لا تَعْلَمُونَ لجهلكم بعواقب أموركم.\nالإشارة: الجهاد على قسمين: جهاد أصغر وهو جهاد السيف، وجهاد أكبر وهو جهاد النفس، فيجاهدها أولاً في القيام بجميع المأمورات، وترك جميع المنهيات، ثم يجاهدها ثانياً في ترك العوائد والشهوات، ومجانبة الرخص والتأويلات، ثم يجاهدها ثالثاً في ترك التدبير والاختيار، والسكون تحت مجاري الأقدار، حتى لا تختار إلا ما اختار الحق تعالى لها، ولا تشتهي إلا ما يقضي الله عليها، فإن النفس جاهلة بالعواقب، فعسى أن تكره شيئاً وهو خير لها، وعسى أن تحب شيئاً وهو شر لها.\nفعسى أن تأتيها المسار من حيث تعتقد المضارّ، وعسى أن تأتيها المضار من حيث ترجو المسار، وعسى أن تنتفع على أيدي الأعداء، وعسى أن تضر على أيدي الأحباء، وعسى أن تكره الموت وهو خير لها، وعسى أن تحب الحياة وهي شر لها، فالواجب تسليم الأمور إلى خالقها، الذي هو عالم بمصالحها، وَاللَّهُ يَعْلَمُ وَأَنْتُمْ لا تَعْلَمُونَ، وهذا كله قبل تصفيتها وكمالها، وأما إذا تهذبت وكملت رياضتها، فالواجب اتباع ما يتجلى فيها، إذ لا يتجلى فيها إلا الحق، وهذا هو ثمرة الجهاد الأكبر، وأما الجهاد الأصغر فلا يحصل شيء من هذا، فلذلك كان مفضولا عند أهل الجهاد الأكبر(1) . وبالله التوفيق.\nولمَّا كان القتال محرماً في الأشهر الحرم في أول الإسلام، ووقع من بعض الصحابة، فندموا وتحرجوا، أزال الله ذلك الحرج عنهم، فقال:","quran_ar":"كُتِبَ عَلَيْكُمُ الْقِتالُ وَهُوَ كُرْهٌ لَكُمْ وَعَسى أَنْ تَكْرَهُوا شَيْئاً وَهُوَ خَيْرٌ لَكُمْ وَعَسى أَنْ تُحِبُّوا شَيْئاً وَهُوَ شَرٌّ لَكُمْ وَاللَّهُ يَعْلَمُ وَأَنْتُمْ لا تَعْلَمُونَ (216)","quran_en":"216. Fighting is prescribed for you, although it is hateful to you. You may dislike a thing that is good for you, and love a thing that is bad for you. God knows, and you do not know.","segments":[{"type":"language","title":"Hardship and aversion","html":"<p>I say: <i>kurh<\/i> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">كُره<\/bdi>), with <i>ḍamma<\/i>, names what is hard upon the self; with <i>fatḥa<\/i>, <i>karh<\/i>, it is the verbal noun (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">المصدر<\/bdi>).<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"I say: kurh ( كُره ), with ḍamma , names what is hard upon the self; with fatḥa , karh , it is the verbal noun ( المصدر )."},{"type":"exposition","title":"Duties, desires and their outcomes","html":"<p>The Real, glorious is His majesty, says: fighting in His path has been made obligatory for you. It is difficult for you and your selves dislike it, although it holds great good for you. You may dislike something that is good for you. Fighting brings victory for your religion, raises the word of your Islam, brings booty and victory over your enemy, and great reward with your Lord. Whoever dies is a martyr; whoever lives lives happily. So too with the other religious obligations (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">التكاليف<\/bdi>): the self dislikes undertaking them, although its soundness depends upon them and they cause its flourishing.<\/p><p>You may love something that is bad for you. You may love rest and abandoning the struggle, although this brings your abasement, the enemy’s ascendancy over you, loss of reward from your Lord and deprivation of martyrdom’s rank with Him. So too with all prohibited things: the self naturally loves and greedily desires them, although they lead to its abasement and humiliation.<\/p><p>The Real uses “may” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">عسى<\/bdi>) because, when the self is disciplined, its condition is reversed: obedience becomes easy and disobedience difficult. God knows what serves your well-being, while you do not know, because you are ignorant of your affairs’ outcomes.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real, glorious is His majesty, says: fighting in His path has been made obligatory for you. It is difficult for you and your selves dislike it, although it holds great good for you. You may dislike something that is good for you. Fighting brings victory for your religion, raises the word of your Islam, brings booty and victory over your enemy, and great reward with your Lord. Whoever dies is a martyr; whoever lives lives happily. So too with the other religious obligations ( التكاليف ): the self dislikes undertaking them, although its soundness depends upon them and they cause its flourishing. You may love something that is bad for you. You may love rest and abandoning the struggle, although this brings your abasement, the enemy’s ascendancy over you, loss of reward from your Lord and deprivation of martyrdom’s rank with Him. So too with all prohibited things: the self naturally loves and greedily desires them, although they lead to its abasement and humiliation. The Real uses “may” ( عسى ) because, when the self is disciplined, its condition is reversed: obedience becomes easy and disobedience difficult. God knows what serves your well-being, while you do not know, because you are ignorant of your affairs’ outcomes."},{"type":"spiritual","title":"The successive struggles against the lower self","html":"<p>Struggle (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الجهاد<\/bdi>) is of two kinds: the lesser struggle, with the sword, and the greater struggle, against the lower self. One first struggles against it to fulfil every command and abandon every prohibition. Secondly, one struggles to abandon habitual comforts and desires and avoid concessions and accommodating interpretations (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الرخص والتأويلات<\/bdi>). Thirdly, one struggles to abandon self-directed management and choice (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">التدبير والاختيار<\/bdi>), resting beneath the flow of decrees until the self chooses only what the Real chooses for it and desires only what God decrees for it. The self is ignorant of outcomes: it may dislike what is good for it and love what is bad for it.<\/p><p>Joys may come from where it expects harms, and harms from where it hopes for joys. It may benefit at enemies’ hands and suffer at loved ones’ hands. It may dislike death although death is good for it, and love life although life is bad for it. What is required is to entrust affairs to their Creator, who knows what benefits them. God knows, and you do not know.<\/p><p>All this precedes the self’s purification and perfection. When it has been refined and its discipline completed, however, one must follow what discloses itself within it, for only the Real discloses Himself there. This is the fruit of the greater struggle. None of this is attained through the lesser struggle; hence it is surpassed in the eyes of the people of the greater struggle. And success is through God.<a class=\"source-marker\" href=\"Source_Reference_Ledger_XII.html#LEGACY-154\" title=\"Inherited reference occurrence; see current source status\">[B149]<\/a><\/p>","authorial":true,"text":"Struggle ( الجهاد ) is of two kinds: the lesser struggle, with the sword, and the greater struggle, against the lower self. One first struggles against it to fulfil every command and abandon every prohibition. Secondly, one struggles to abandon habitual comforts and desires and avoid concessions and accommodating interpretations ( الرخص والتأويلات ). Thirdly, one struggles to abandon self-directed management and choice ( التدبير والاختيار ), resting beneath the flow of decrees until the self chooses only what the Real chooses for it and desires only what God decrees for it. The self is ignorant of outcomes: it may dislike what is good for it and love what is bad for it. Joys may come from where it expects harms, and harms from where it hopes for joys. It may benefit at enemies’ hands and suffer at loved ones’ hands. It may dislike death although death is good for it, and love life although life is bad for it. What is required is to entrust affairs to their Creator, who knows what benefits them. God knows, and you do not know. All this precedes the self’s purification and perfection. When it has been refined and its discipline completed, however, one must follow what discloses itself within it, for only the Real discloses Himself there. This is the fruit of the greater struggle. None of this is attained through the lesser struggle; hence it is surpassed in the eyes of the people of the greater struggle. And success is through God. [B149]"},{"type":"transition","title":"","html":"<p>At the beginning of Islam, fighting was forbidden in the sacred months. Some Companions fought then, regretted it and felt distress; God removed that distress from them.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"At the beginning of Islam, fighting was forbidden in the sacred months. Some Companions fought then, regretted it and felt distress; God removed that distress from them."}],"cairo_notes":[{"id":"C243.1","page":243,"ar":"لا يعني هذا الغض من جهاد أعداء الدين، وقد كان للصوفية فيه دور كبير مهم.","en":"This does not mean belittling the struggle against the enemies of religion; the Sufis have played a large and important role in it.","anchor":"فلذلك كان مفضولا عند أهل الجهاد الأكبر"}],"english_characters":2991,"arabic_characters":2212},{"id":"q2-217-218","q":"2:217–218","s":2,"surah":"Al-Baqara","title":"2:217–218 · Fighting in the sacred month, expulsion and steadfastness in faith","source":"Cairo print · Volume I, pp. 243–245","cairo":"I:243–245","source_page_first":243,"source_page_last":245,"ar":"يَسْئَلُونَكَ عَنِ الشَّهْرِ الْحَرامِ قِتالٍ فِيهِ قُلْ قِتالٌ فِيهِ كَبِيرٌ وَصَدٌّ عَنْ سَبِيلِ اللَّهِ وَكُفْرٌ بِهِ وَالْمَسْجِدِ الْحَرامِ وَإِخْراجُ أَهْلِهِ مِنْهُ أَكْبَرُ عِنْدَ اللَّهِ وَالْفِتْنَةُ أَكْبَرُ مِنَ الْقَتْلِ وَلا يَزالُونَ يُقاتِلُونَكُمْ حَتَّى يَرُدُّوكُمْ عَنْ دِينِكُمْ إِنِ اسْتَطاعُوا وَمَنْ يَرْتَدِدْ مِنْكُمْ عَنْ دِينِهِ فَيَمُتْ وَهُوَ كافِرٌ فَأُولئِكَ حَبِطَتْ أَعْمالُهُمْ فِي الدُّنْيا وَالْآخِرَةِ وَأُولئِكَ أَصْحابُ النَّارِ هُمْ فِيها خالِدُونَ (217) إِنَّ الَّذِينَ آمَنُوا وَالَّذِينَ هاجَرُوا وَجاهَدُوا فِي سَبِيلِ اللَّهِ أُولئِكَ يَرْجُونَ رَحْمَتَ اللَّهِ وَاللَّهُ غَفُورٌ رَحِيمٌ (218)\nقلت: (قتال) : بدل اشتمال من (الشهر الحرام) ، وقد وقع خبط في عطف (المسجد الحرام،) والصواب: ما قاله الزمخشري وابن عطية أنه عطف على (السبيل) إذ هو المتبادر من جهة المعنى، أي: وصد عن سبيل الله وعن المسجد الحرام أكبر جرماً من قتل السَّرِية في الشهر الحرام، والقواعد النحوية إنما هى أغلبية.\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: يَسْئَلُونَكَ يا محمد عَنِ الشَّهْرِ الْحَرامِ أي: عن القتال فى الأشهر الحرم، قُلْ لهم: القتالُ في الشهر الحرام أمره كَبِيرٌ، لكن ما وقع من الكفار من صد الناس عَنْ سَبِيلِ اللَّهِ أي:\nمنعهم من الإسلام والطاعة، وكذلك كفرهم بالله وصدهم المسلمين عن الْمَسْجِدِ الْحَرامِ عام الحديبية، وإخراج المسلمين من مكة التي هي بلدهم- وَالْفِتْنَةُ التي هم فيها من الكفر، وافتتان الناس عن دينهم- أَكْبَرُ جرماً من القتال الذي وقع في الشهر الحرام تأويلاً وظنّاً أنه لم يدخل الشهر الحرام.\nوذلك أن النبي صلى الله عليه وسلم بعث سرية وأمّر عليها عبد الله بنَ جَحْش في آخر جُمَادى الآخِرَةِ، فَلَقُوا عمرو بن الحضرمي، مع أناس من قريش، بعد غروب الشمس من جمادى الآخرة، فرموا عمراً فقتلوه، وأخذوا الغنيمة، فقال لهم عليه الصلاة والسلام: «لم آمركم أن تقتلوا في الشهر الحرام» فندموا، وبعثت قريش بالعتاب للنبيّ صلّى الله عليه وسلم: كيف تستحل القتال في الشهر الحرام؟ فنزلت هذه الآية. ثم نسخ تحريم القتال في الأشهر الحرم بقوله تعالى: وَقاتِلُوا الْمُشْرِكِينَ كَافَّةً كَما يُقاتِلُونَكُمْ كَافَّةً.\nثم قال الحق جلّ جلاله في التحذير من الكفار: وَلا يَزالُونَ يُقاتِلُونَكُمْ حَتَّى يَرُدُّوكُمْ عَنْ دِينِكُمْ إِنِ اسْتَطاعُوا، لكن لا يطيقون ذلك، وَمَنْ يَرْتَدِدْ مِنْكُمْ عَنْ دِينِهِ ويستمر عليه حتى يموت وَهُوَ كافِرٌ فَأُولئِكَ حَبِطَتْ أَعْمالُهُمْ فِي الدُّنْيا فلا حرمة له، ولا نصيب له في الفيء والغنيمة، وفي الْآخِرَةِ فلا يرى لها ثواباً، وَأُولئِكَ أَصْحابُ النَّارِ هُمْ فِيها خالِدُونَ.\nومفهوم الآية: أنه إن رجع قبل الموت لا يحبَط عملُه، وهو قول الشافعي. وقال مالك: يحبط أجر كل ما عمل، ويعيد الحج، إن تقدم على الردة، ويقبل منه الإسلام إن رجع، فإنْ لم يرجع أمهل ثلاثة أيام، ثم يقتل.\nولمّا نزلت الآية في إسقاط الحرج، ظنوا أنه لا أجر لهم في ذلك الجهاد، فأنزل الحق جل جلاله: إِنَّ الَّذِينَ آمَنُوا وَالَّذِينَ هاجَرُوا وَجاهَدُوا فِي سَبِيلِ اللَّهِ أُولئِكَ يَرْجُونَ رَحْمَتَ اللَّهِ أي ثوابه، وَاللَّهُ غَفُورٌ لهم رَحِيمٌ بهم، فلا يضيع جهادهم في هذه السرِيَّة، وأعاد الموصول لتعظيم شأن الهجرة والجهاد، وعبَّر بالرجاء إشعاراً بأن العمل غير موجب للثواب، وإنما هو عبودية، والأمر بيد الله إن شاء أثاب وإن شاء عاقب، لاَ يُسْئَلُ عَمَّا يَفْعَلُ وَهُمْ يُسْئَلُونَ.\nالإشارة: تعظيم الزمان والمكان يكون بقدر ما يقع فيه من طاعة الملك الديان، فالزمان الذي تهب فيه نفحات القبول والإقبال، لا ينبغي أن يقع فيه ملاججة ولا قتال، وهو وقت حضرة الذكر، أو التذكير، أو الجلوس مع\nالعارفين أهل الإكسير، فسوء الأدب فيه أمره كبير، ومنع القاصدين من وصوله جُرمه كبير، وصد القلوب عن نفحات تلك الحضرة أكبر من كل كبير، ولا يزال قُطَّاع هذه الطريق يردون من أراد سلوكها على التحقيق، لكن من سبق له التأييد لا يرده عن الحق جبار ولا عنيد، ومن سبق له الحرمان، وحَكم عليه القضاءُ بالخذلان، رجع ولو بعد العيان، وأنشدوا:\nوالله ما نَشْكُرْ خَلِيعْ ... وإنْ ثَمِلْ. وإن صَحَا\nوإن ثَبَتْ، سَيْرٌ سَرِيعْ ... وإن شَرِبْ حَتَّى امْتَحا\nحَتى يُقَطَّعْ في القَطيعْ ... ويَدُورْ دَوْرَ الرَحَا(1)\nإن الذين آمنوا وصدَّقُوا بطريق الله، وهاجروا أهواءهم في مرضاة الله، وجاهدوا نفوسهم في محبة الله، أولئك يرجون رحمة الله، فلا يُخيبهم الكريم لأنه غفور رحيم.\nولمّا كان الخمر حلالاً في أول الإسلام، وكانوا يشربونه، ويتِّجُرون فيه، فيتصدقون بثمنه وبثمن القمار، بيَّن الحقّ تعالى ذلك، بعد الأمر بالإنفاق لئلا يقع التساهل في المعاملة بعلّة الصدقة، فقال:","quran_ar":"يَسْئَلُونَكَ عَنِ الشَّهْرِ الْحَرامِ قِتالٍ فِيهِ قُلْ قِتالٌ فِيهِ كَبِيرٌ وَصَدٌّ عَنْ سَبِيلِ اللَّهِ وَكُفْرٌ بِهِ وَالْمَسْجِدِ الْحَرامِ وَإِخْراجُ أَهْلِهِ مِنْهُ أَكْبَرُ عِنْدَ اللَّهِ وَالْفِتْنَةُ أَكْبَرُ مِنَ الْقَتْلِ وَلا يَزالُونَ يُقاتِلُونَكُمْ حَتَّى يَرُدُّوكُمْ عَنْ دِينِكُمْ إِنِ اسْتَطاعُوا وَمَنْ يَرْتَدِدْ مِنْكُمْ عَنْ دِينِهِ فَيَمُتْ وَهُوَ كافِرٌ فَأُولئِكَ حَبِطَتْ أَعْمالُهُمْ فِي الدُّنْيا وَالْآخِرَةِ وَأُولئِكَ أَصْحابُ النَّارِ هُمْ فِيها خالِدُونَ (217) إِنَّ الَّذِينَ آمَنُوا وَالَّذِينَ هاجَرُوا وَجاهَدُوا فِي سَبِيلِ اللَّهِ أُولئِكَ يَرْجُونَ رَحْمَتَ اللَّهِ وَاللَّهُ غَفُورٌ رَحِيمٌ (218)","quran_en":"217. They ask you about the sacred month—fighting in it. Say, “Fighting in it is grave; but barring people from God’s path, unbelief in Him, barring access to the Sacred Mosque and expelling its people from it are graver before God. Unbelief and turning people away from their religion (fitna) are graver than killing.” They will continue fighting you until they turn you back from your religion, if they can. Whoever among you turns back from his religion and dies an unbeliever—those people’s works have come to nothing in this world and the Hereafter. They are the inhabitants of the Fire, remaining in it. 218. Those who believe, and those who emigrate and strive in God’s path, hope for God’s mercy. God is Forgiving, Merciful.","segments":[{"type":"language","title":"An inclusive substitution and a disputed coordination","html":"<p>I say: “Fighting” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">قتال<\/bdi>) is a substitution of inclusion (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">بدل اشتمال<\/bdi>) for “the sacred month.” There has been confusion concerning the coordination (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">العطف<\/bdi>) of “the Sacred Mosque.” The correct explanation is that of al-Zamakhsharī and Ibn ʿAṭiyya: it is coordinated with “the path,” since this is the meaning that immediately suggests itself—barring from God’s path and from the Sacred Mosque is a greater offence than the expedition’s killing during the sacred month. Grammatical rules describe what generally obtains; they are not without exceptions.<\/p><p class=\"translator-note-ref\">[Translator’s print-source notes: <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/note/R07-09\">[R07-09]<\/a>]<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"I say: “Fighting” ( قتال ) is a substitution of inclusion ( بدل اشتمال ) for “the sacred month.” There has been confusion concerning the coordination ( العطف ) of “the Sacred Mosque.” The correct explanation is that of al-Zamakhsharī and Ibn ʿAṭiyya: it is coordinated with “the path,” since this is the meaning that immediately suggests itself—barring from God’s path and from the Sacred Mosque is a greater offence than the expedition’s killing during the sacred month. Grammatical rules describe what generally obtains; they are not without exceptions. [Translator’s print-source notes: [R07-09] ]"},{"type":"exposition","title":"The expedition and the opponents’ greater wrongs","html":"<p>The Real, glorious is His majesty, says: they ask you, Muḥammad, about the sacred month—that is, fighting during the sacred months. Tell them: fighting during the sacred month is a grave matter. Yet the disbelievers’ barring people from God’s path—preventing their entry into Islam and obedience—their unbelief in God, their barring Muslims from the Sacred Mosque in the year of al-Ḥudaybiya, and their expulsion of Muslims from Mecca, their own homeland, together with the <i>fitna<\/i> of their unbelief and turning people from their religion, are graver offences than the fighting that occurred in the sacred month through an interpretation and the supposition that the sacred month had not yet begun.<\/p><p>The Prophet, God bless him and grant him peace, had sent an expedition under ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaḥsh at the end of Jumādā al-Ākhira. They encountered ʿAmr ibn al-Ḥaḍramī with a group of Quraysh after sunset on [the final day of] Jumādā al-Ākhira, shot ʿAmr, killed him and took the booty. The Prophet told them: “I did not command you to kill in the sacred month.” They regretted it. Quraysh sent a reproach to the Prophet: “How can you consider fighting in the sacred month lawful?” This verse was revealed. The prohibition of fighting during the sacred months was then abrogated by His saying, “Fight the associators all together, just as they fight you all together” [9:36].<a class=\"source-marker\" href=\"Source_Reference_Ledger_XII.html#LEGACY-155\" title=\"Inherited reference occurrence; see current source status\">[B150]<\/a><\/p><p>The Real then warns against the disbelievers: they will continue fighting you until they turn you back from your religion, if they can—but they cannot accomplish that. Whoever among you turns back from his religion and persists until he dies an unbeliever, his works become void in this world: he has no protected standing and no share in revenue or booty. In the Hereafter he sees no reward for them. Such people are inhabitants of the Fire, remaining there.<\/p><p>The verse’s implication (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">مفهوم الآية<\/bdi>) is that, if he returns before death, his work does not become void. This is al-Shāfiʿī’s position. Mālik says that the reward of everything he did becomes void; he repeats pilgrimage if it preceded his apostasy. His Islam is accepted if he returns. If he does not return, he is allowed three days and then put to death.<a class=\"source-marker\" href=\"Source_Reference_Ledger_XII.html#LEGACY-156\" title=\"Inherited reference occurrence; see current source status\">[B151]<\/a><\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real, glorious is His majesty, says: they ask you, Muḥammad, about the sacred month—that is, fighting during the sacred months. Tell them: fighting during the sacred month is a grave matter. Yet the disbelievers’ barring people from God’s path—preventing their entry into Islam and obedience—their unbelief in God, their barring Muslims from the Sacred Mosque in the year of al-Ḥudaybiya, and their expulsion of Muslims from Mecca, their own homeland, together with the fitna of their unbelief and turning people from their religion, are graver offences than the fighting that occurred in the sacred month through an interpretation and the supposition that the sacred month had not yet begun. The Prophet, God bless him and grant him peace, had sent an expedition under ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaḥsh at the end of Jumādā al-Ākhira. They encountered ʿAmr ibn al-Ḥaḍramī with a group of Quraysh after sunset on [the final day of] Jumādā al-Ākhira, shot ʿAmr, killed him and took the booty. The Prophet told them: “I did not command you to kill in the sacred month.” They regretted it. Quraysh sent a reproach to the Prophet: “How can you consider fighting in the sacred month lawful?” This verse was revealed. The prohibition of fighting during the sacred months was then abrogated by His saying, “Fight the associators all together, just as they fight you all together” [9:36]. [B150] The Real then warns against the disbelievers: they will continue fighting you until they turn you back from your religion, if they can—but they cannot accomplish that. Whoever among you turns back from his religion and persists until he dies an unbeliever, his works become void in this world: he has no protected standing and no share in revenue or booty. In the Hereafter he sees no reward for them. Such people are inhabitants of the Fire, remaining there. The verse’s implication ( مفهوم الآية ) is that, if he returns before death, his work does not become void. This is al-Shāfiʿī’s position. Mālik says that the reward of everything he did becomes void; he repeats pilgrimage if it preceded his apostasy. His Islam is accepted if he returns. If he does not return, he is allowed three days and then put to death. [B151]"},{"type":"theology","title":"Hope is not an entitlement earned against God","html":"<p>When the verse removing blame was revealed, they thought they had no reward for that fighting. The Real therefore revealed: those who believe, and those who emigrate and strive in God’s path, hope for God’s mercy—His reward. God is Forgiving towards them, Merciful to them, and does not waste their striving in this expedition. The relative expression is repeated to magnify the standing of emigration and struggle. He uses hope to indicate that work does not make reward obligatory: it is servanthood. The affair rests with God; He rewards if He wills and punishes if He wills. “He is not questioned about what He does, but they are questioned” [21:23].<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"When the verse removing blame was revealed, they thought they had no reward for that fighting. The Real therefore revealed: those who believe, and those who emigrate and strive in God’s path, hope for God’s mercy—His reward. God is Forgiving towards them, Merciful to them, and does not waste their striving in this expedition. The relative expression is repeated to magnify the standing of emigration and struggle. He uses hope to indicate that work does not make reward obligatory: it is servanthood. The affair rests with God; He rewards if He wills and punishes if He wills. “He is not questioned about what He does, but they are questioned” [21:23]."},{"type":"spiritual","title":"The sanctity of a gathering and the danger of turning back","html":"<p>The honour of a time or place is measured by the obedience to the King, the Requiter, that occurs in it. A time when the breezes of acceptance and turning towards God blow should not be a time of wrangling or fighting. This is the time of a gathering for remembrance or reminder, or sitting with<\/p><p>the gnostics, possessors of the elixir. Bad comportment there is grave; preventing those who seek it from reaching it is a grave offence; barring hearts from the breaths of that presence is graver still.<\/p><p>The brigands of this path never cease turning back those who wish truly to follow it. Yet no tyrant or obstinate person can turn from the Real one for whom divine support has preceded. One for whom deprivation preceded and abandonment was decreed turns back, even after direct vision. They recite:<\/p><blockquote class=\"poetry\">By God, we praise no unbridled one,<br>whether drunk or sober;<br>even if firmly set upon a swift journey,<br>even if he drinks until effaced—<br>not until he is cut down within the flock<br>and turns with the turning of a millstone.<a class=\"source-marker\" href=\"Source_Reference_Ledger_XII.html#LEGACY-157\" title=\"Inherited reference occurrence; see current source status\">[B152]<\/a><\/blockquote><p>Those who believe and affirm God’s path, leave behind their desires to please God, and struggle against their selves in love of God—those hope for God’s mercy. The Generous One will not disappoint them, for He is Forgiving, Merciful.<\/p>","authorial":true,"text":"The honour of a time or place is measured by the obedience to the King, the Requiter, that occurs in it. A time when the breezes of acceptance and turning towards God blow should not be a time of wrangling or fighting. This is the time of a gathering for remembrance or reminder, or sitting with the gnostics, possessors of the elixir. Bad comportment there is grave; preventing those who seek it from reaching it is a grave offence; barring hearts from the breaths of that presence is graver still. The brigands of this path never cease turning back those who wish truly to follow it. Yet no tyrant or obstinate person can turn from the Real one for whom divine support has preceded. One for whom deprivation preceded and abandonment was decreed turns back, even after direct vision. They recite: By God, we praise no unbridled one, whether drunk or sober; even if firmly set upon a swift journey, even if he drinks until effaced— not until he is cut down within the flock and turns with the turning of a millstone. [B152] Those who believe and affirm God’s path, leave behind their desires to please God, and struggle against their selves in love of God—those hope for God’s mercy. The Generous One will not disappoint them, for He is Forgiving, Merciful."},{"type":"transition","title":"","html":"<p>Wine was lawful at the beginning of Islam. People drank and traded in it, giving its proceeds and gambling winnings in charity. After commanding expenditure, the Real clarifies this, so that charity does not become a pretext for laxity in dealings.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"Wine was lawful at the beginning of Islam. People drank and traded in it, giving its proceeds and gambling winnings in charity. After commanding expenditure, the Real clarifies this, so that charity does not become a pretext for laxity in dealings."}],"cairo_notes":[{"id":"C245.1","page":245,"ar":"زجل الششتري.","en":"A zajal by al-Shushtarī.","anchor":"ويَدُورْ دَوْرَ الرَحَا"}],"english_characters":4964,"arabic_characters":4025}];
